// THE ONLY CI EMISSION THIS REPOSITORY HAS.
//
// Authored fresh on 2026-08-15 as re-add #1 after the CI wipe, deliberately NOT by reviving
// gunbc.ci_workflow's job-spec architecture. That module emitted six jobs across a build -> regen
// -> ci -> heal -> fleet pipeline with per-step timeout rows, artifact transfer between jobs,
// concurrency expressions, upstream-red refusal arms and a floor step whose plan resolved batches,
// workers and a coordinator. None of it is re-derived here, because none of it follows from the
// obligation this workflow discharges.
//
// The obligation is one sentence: every test witness in the repository executes, on every push
// and pull request, and the run is red if any of them is. From that, one job: check out the tree,
// install the toolchain, build the one binary that folds the witnesses, run it once. No second
// job, so nothing to sequence, no artifact to hand off, no `needs` edge to get wrong.
//
// WHAT IS DELIBERATELY ABSENT, so a later reader does not restore it as an oversight: heal, the
// generated-artifact drift gates, the seven effect gates, the fmt gate, merge-admission stamping
// and the falsifier cadence are all real obligations and all currently unmet. They return one at
// a time, each re-derived from its own first principles under its own operator agreement. Their
// absence is the declared state of the ladder, not a gap in this module.
//
// REGEN WAS ABSENT FOR A DIFFERENT REASON THAN THE REST OF THE QUEUE, AND IS NOW BACK (corrected
// 2026-08-19; supersedes three paragraphs that reasoned about a still-open integration/v1-cut race
// between two candidate cuts). Neither candidate is what happened: integration/v1-cut never
// reached main, and the v1 .dag compiler authority was never deleted. Instead the REGEN ROOT CUT
// (#8406, 3b431f34a9e, 2026-08-18) deleted the old `regen_stage0` binary and its
// RegenVerifyGate/SelfHostStalenessGate CI gates, and rebuilt required-regen as one fold in the
// same binary this job already runs: `claim_executor --required-regen` and
// `--required-regen-fixed-point`, backed by src/v1/stage0/src/required_regen_host.rs. The fold
// still calls the live v1 .dag compiler to regenerate stage0's Rust sources and compares the
// result against the committed, frozen src/v1/stage0/src/*.rs files -- its subject was never
// deleted, only its old CI harness.
//
// So the gap is now a plain one: the fold exists and has never been invoked from CI. The two
// steps below close it by adding two more invocations of the one binary already built for the
// floor fold, in the same job, after it -- no second job, no artifact hand-off, no new `needs`
// edge, for the same reason the header gives for not reviving gunbc.ci_workflow.
//
// THE DRIFT GATES DID NOT RIDE ON THE OLD REGEN EITHER, and still don't: regen failing is a
// self-host fixed-point defect (the emitted stage0 sources diverge from what the compiler would
// emit today), materially different from the generated-artifact drift gate (which diffs THIS
// repository's checked-in generated files against what THIS commit's .dag sources would emit for
// THEM). The two steps below add a third, unrelated failure surface to the job; they do not
// change what the drift gate covers.

// THE REQUIRED CONTEXT'S NAME, AND AS OF 2026-08-25 IT NAMES THE AGGREGATION JOB RATHER THAN THE
// FLOOR JOB. The distinction is the whole of what keeps the split fail-closed.
//
// WHAT WAS WRONG WITH THE FIRST CUT OF THE SPLIT, measured (found in review of gunbc#9203, and
// confirmed against the live ruleset rather than the workflow): the repository's `passing CI`
// ruleset is ACTIVE and names exactly ONE required status check -- `witnesses`. THIS CLAUSE
// PREVIOUSLY ALSO READ `has NO bypass actors`, AND THAT IS NOT A FACT A SESSION CAN STATE:
// `bypass_actors` and `current_user_can_bypass` are answered RELATIVE TO THE CREDENTIAL ASKING,
// and this repository's sessions authenticate as an integration rather than a user. Read through
// a user credential the same ruleset reports a `RepositoryRole` bypass at `bypass_mode=always`.
// Both readings are correct about different subjects, and the emptier one was taken twice through
// the same kind of credential and mistaken for corroboration. Nothing here rests on it -- every
// measurement was taken against runs and check-runs -- so the clause is removed rather than
// repaired. A GitHub required context is produced by the JOB, not the workflow, so a second job
// publishes its own check under its own name and that check is not required. The first cut
// therefore moved regen and v2-emission into a `build` job whose failure COULD NOT BLOCK A MERGE:
// the required `witnesses` check would go green over a regen drift or a v2 emission break, and
// the PR would be mergeable. That is fail-open (DESIGN §5) -- and worse than the serial run it
// replaced, whose single job carried every phase into the one context that gates.
//
// WHY AN AGGREGATION JOB AND NOT A RULESET EDIT. No wall inside this repository can constrain what
// GitHub admits to main (the boundary the merge_group note below records). Landing a change whose
// safety depends on someone editing a setting afterwards is a coverage gap with a promise
// attached, and a real window: until the context is added, the lane is unguarded. An aggregation
// job needs no setting change and no window -- the context that already gates keeps its name and
// now answers for BOTH lanes.
//
// THAT ARGUMENT SURVIVED A BOUNDARY MOVE AND ITS FIRST CLAUSE DID NOT. This note read `the ruleset
// is not a .dag fact`, and it is one now: gunbc.repo_ruleset declares the desired ruleset,
// observes the live one, reconciles, applies and READS BACK, and imports the required context's
// name from witness_floor_workflow_job_id below rather than spelling it again -- so renaming this
// job can no longer leave the ruleset naming a context nothing publishes, the failure that shows
// up as a check stuck pending rather than red. What did NOT change is why the aggregation job
// exists: a converged authority ACTUATES and DETECTS, it does not gate, so it still cannot make a
// required context exist at the moment a merge is attempted. The window argument stands.
//
// WHAT IT COSTS, and it is not the parallelism. The two lane jobs still carry no `needs` edge on
// each other and still start together; only the aggregator waits, and it does nothing but read
// two results. Each lane job's log is findable by its lane name, and the aggregator's own output
// names which lane to open.
data witness_floor_workflow_job_id: String = "witnesses"

// THE LANE JOB IDS ARE THEIR OWN DECLARATIONS, AND UN-FUSING THEM FROM THE LANE WORDS IS THE WHOLE
// OF THIS CHANGE.
//
// WHY A UNIQUE NAME IS NEEDED AT ALL. A GitHub required status check names a CONTEXT, and a
// context is the job's name -- which workflow published it is not part of the name. So `build` is
// not a name this repository owns: `gunbc.fleet_converge_workflow` declares a job whose id is also
// exactly `build`, and a rule naming `build` names two jobs in two workflows, answered by
// whichever green arrives. Enforcement cannot move onto a lane job until that lane's context is
// unique across every workflow emitted here.
//
// WHY THE NAME IS NOT COMPUTED FROM THE LANE WORD -- the modeling content, not a spelling
// preference. `build_lane_job_id` was DEFINED AS `required_ci_lane_build`, which read as single
// authority because the two strings coincided. They are two facts with different authorities and
// consumers: the LANE WORD is a CLI argument this workflow passes to `claim_executor
// --required-lane`, owned by `gunbc.fabric_witness_run` and consumed by an exhaustive
// `RequiredCiPhase::lane` match inside the binary; the JOB ID is a GitHub required-context name,
// owned here and consumed by a repository ruleset that is not a `.dag` fact at all. Their
// coincidence was the accident, not the authority. Deriving the job id from the lane word --
// including by concatenating a prefix -- re-fuses them through a different door and in the
// dangerous direction: a lane rename, an ordinary CLI-vocabulary edit, would silently move a
// REQUIRED CONTEXT NAME and a check would stop being required with nothing going red. The rows
// below are therefore spelled, and every reference to a job id in this file is taken FROM these
// declarations rather than typed, so a rename here moves the `needs` list and the `needs.*.result`
// expressions with it.
//
// WHAT THIS DOES NOT DO: move enforcement. `witnesses` remains the aggregation job and the one
// required context the ruleset names, produced exactly as before, so the gate is unchanged and no
// window opens. The lane contexts are published and ignored until an operator names them in the
// ruleset -- a repository setting no wall in this repository can constrain. Until that edit lands
// the aggregator is still what gates; after it, the aggregator's role is diagnosis rather than
// enforcement, because a same-run dependent job is SKIPPED when a lane fails or the run is
// cancelled at run level, and a SKIPPED required context does not block a merge (measured:
// `mergeable=MERGEABLE mergeStateStatus=UNSTABLE`), while root jobs carrying no `needs` edge take
// cancelled/success/failure and are never skipped.
data floor_lane_job_id: String = "required-witnesses-floor"

data build_lane_job_id: String = "required-witnesses-build"

data heal_generated_artifacts_job_id: String = "heal-generated-artifacts"

// The workflow's own name, hoisted to a declaration because a second consumer now joins on
// it. gunbc.fleet_desired_admission refuses a workflow_run payload whose workflow_name is
// not the required floor's; spelling "witnesses" a second time there would fork the name
// (DESIGN §3) and the fork would fail open, not loud — a renamed workflow would leave the
// admission comparing against a name nothing produces, refusing every candidate forever.
data witness_floor_workflow_name: String = "witnesses"

// CANCELLATION IS A PER-EVENT FACT, AND THE GROUP CARRIES HALF OF IT. Operator ruling 2026-08-17:
// every push to main must finish; only pull-request runs may be superseded. The predecessor
// collapsed both axes into one group keyed on `github.ref` with an unconditional `true`, so
// consecutive pushes to main landed in the single group `witness-floor-refs/heads/main` and each
// new push killed the previous commit's run -- main's floor coverage was decided by push arrival,
// the empty-observation narrow section 5 forbids: a cancelled run reports no verdict, and no
// verdict was being read as nothing to report.
//
// Setting `cancel_in_progress` false for main is NOT sufficient on its own. GitHub cancels PENDING
// runs in a group regardless of that flag -- it only protects a run that has already started -- so
// a main run queued behind a busy fleet could still be superseded before it executed. The group
// must therefore be unique per main run, which `github.run_id` gives, and the flag must
// additionally be false there so the two mechanisms agree rather than one relying on the other.
//
// The cost is that N concurrent main runs may execute where one used to. That is the intended
// trade -- coverage of every landed commit, at fleet occupancy -- and the reason the dep-info race
// under `target/` is worth identifying before this is widened further.
//
// AND QUEUEING IS NOT THE THIRD ARM IT LOOKS LIKE. Sharing one main group with cancellation off
// makes GitHub QUEUE same-group runs rather than drop them, which reads as coverage without
// concurrency cost -- but the floor's merge-critical wall runs hours against a main push gap of
// minutes (run 31672107941, the last main run to complete before the 2026-08-13 measurement), so a
// queued policy accumulates an unbounded backlog and delivers each verdict long after the commit it
// judges. A unique key runs them concurrently instead, which is the only arm that yields a verdict
// per main commit at this arrival rate. This paragraph is carried here because it was the only
// record of that measurement, and it lived in `gunbc.ci_workflow_expressions` -- a SECOND
// concurrency authority left behind when the floor cut deleted the workflow it fed. That module's
// note asserted main was grouped by `github.sha` and "never run_id", which is not what CI runs;
// its rows are deleted with this one standing as the single authority.
data witness_floor_concurrency_group: String = "witness-floor-${{ github.event.pull_request.number || github.run_id }}"

// SUPERSESSION IS DISABLED, AND THIS ROW IS THE WHOLE POLICY.
//
// This value decides whether GitHub cancels an in-flight run when a newer one joins the group. It
// was `${{ github.event_name == 'pull_request' }}` -- superseding pull-request runs to save fleet
// occupancy. It is now false for every event, and that is a DECLARED, TEMPORARY REGRESSION in
// capacity efficiency taken to stop a capacity LEAK, which is the larger loss.
//
// WHY, MEASURED RATHER THAN ARGUED. The fleet's builds are bounded by a host-persistent GNU
// jobserver whose permits are returned COOPERATIVELY. A cancelled run SIGKILLs cargo mid-build, so
// every borrowed permit that process held is lost for the lifetime of the daemon -- there is no
// action a process can take after SIGKILL. The pool therefore decays monotonically toward zero
// under normal supersession, and a host at zero permits still accepts jobs: they simply wait.
//
// srv1 reached 0 of 128 permits with 86.8% CPU idle and 21 runner jobs resident. Its
// required-witnesses-build median was 26.1 minutes over 11 runs, range 24.8-27.3. Its daemon
// self-restarted on RuntimeMaxSec=86400 at 2026-08-30T22:35:54Z with no human action and reseeded
// 128 permits; the next two builds took 11.0 and 12.2 minutes, and the median over the following
// six was 12.5. The band was registered BEFORE the restart, which is what makes this an
// intervention rather than a correlation -- the earlier host-contention account had already failed
// against the timing, since durations were flat across fleet concurrency 0/30/41/14. srv3, never
// reseeded, still medians 22.8 minutes against srv4's 11.8.
//
// THE COST OF THIS ROW IS N CONCURRENT RUNS PER PULL REQUEST where one used to survive. That is
// the same trade the concurrency group above already accepts for main, for the same reason: stale
// work completing is expensive, and capacity silently disappearing is not recoverable. Cancelling
// is only cheaper than not cancelling while the permits come back.
//
// DISSOLVE-ON, and it is the CAPABILITY rather than any artifact: no client of the required floor
// holds a host-persistent cooperative permit -- either the ctrl host FIFO is deleted and compile
// parallelism is invocation-local to cargo, or the pool is server-owned with admission that
// survives client death. At that point a SIGKILL costs only the work in flight, supersession stops
// destroying capacity, and this row goes back to an expression. Re-enabling it earlier restores
// the leak; re-enabling it as part of the cutover canary is how the cutover proves itself, because
// a cancelled build that leaves the next build at full declared capacity IS the acceptance test.
// THE SUPERSESSION POLICY IS SEMANTIC, AND THE YAML SPELLING IS ITS PROJECTION.
//
// `cancel-in-progress` is a SERIALIZED SPELLING of a business decision, and the decision is a
// workflow fact this repository owns -- DESIGN section 3 puts business policy in the workflow layer
// and calls modelling it in extdeps a layer inversion. So the policy is declared here as a closed
// vocabulary and the extdeps spec is DERIVED from it, never the reverse.
//
// WHY THIS IS NOT COSMETIC: the classifier used to consult the serialized form by asking whether
// the expression text CONTAINED the event name. That is not expression evaluation, and it inverts
// on a perfectly ordinary policy -- `${{ github.event_name != 'pull_request' }}` contains
// `pull_request` while authorizing exactly the opposite population, so a substring reader would
// report supersession available for the one event the policy excludes. Reading the semantic arm
// makes the question decidable by construction instead of by text search.
type RunningSupersessionPolicy
  = NeverSupersedeRunning
  | SupersedeRunningForEvent { event: String }

// THE QUEUE AXIS IS THE SECOND HALF OF THE SAME FACT, and omitting it left the classifier carrying
// a hardcoded copy of today's regime. ConcurrencySpec already distinguishes QueueMax from
// QueueNotMax: under the not-max regime a newly queued run REPLACES the existing pending member,
// which is what makes a pending cancellation lawful whatever cancel-in-progress says. A classifier
// that assumes replacement is universally lawful is asserting QueueNotMax without consulting it,
// and would go on asserting it if this workflow ever moved to the retaining regime.
type PendingReplacementPolicy
  = ReplaceExistingPending
  | RetainPendingQueue

type WitnessFloorConcurrencyPolicy {
  pending: PendingReplacementPolicy
  running: RunningSupersessionPolicy
}

// THE RUNNING AXIS IS DERIVED FROM THE OBSERVED FLEET STANDING, not authored beside it and not read
// off a desired-state row.
//
// gunbc#9789 declared NeverSupersedeRunning and a DESIGN 4b(3) drop whose dissolve-on is a
// capability: no client of the required floor holds a permit whose abandonment costs future compile
// capacity. Written as a literal, that row survives its own reason -- the fleet stops leaking and CI
// goes on refusing to supersede, the drop stays standing, and nothing refuses because nothing joins
// the two facts. Section 4b(3) says a drop is retired BY ITS TRIGGER AND BY NOTHING ELSE, so the
// trigger is the whole check, and a check nothing evaluates is diligence rather than a check.
//
// IT READS THE STANDING AND NOT THE REALIZATION, for a reason that is a safety property rather than
// a taste: a desired realization row and the live hosts are two independently actuated systems, and
// a generated artifact cannot make them transition together. Deriving from the row would restore
// cancellation the moment this workflow reached main, while hosts that had not yet restarted onto
// the new unit went on losing a permit per kill. The unobserved fleet is on the refusing side, so
// that interval is unwritable rather than merely unlikely.
fn running_supersession_for_fleet_standing(
  standing: FleetCompileCancellationStanding,
) -> RunningSupersessionPolicy {
  match standing {
    FleetCancellationNotSafe { unsafe_hosts: _, unobserved_hosts: _ } => NeverSupersedeRunning
    FleetCancellationSafe { hosts: _ } => SupersedeRunningForEvent { event: "pull_request" }
  }
}

data witness_floor_concurrency_policy: WitnessFloorConcurrencyPolicy = WitnessFloorConcurrencyPolicy {
  pending: ReplaceExistingPending,
  running: running_supersession_for_fleet_standing(standing: fleet_compile_cancellation_standing)
}

data witness_floor_running_supersession_policy: RunningSupersessionPolicy = witness_floor_concurrency_policy.running

data witness_floor_pending_replacement_policy: PendingReplacementPolicy = witness_floor_concurrency_policy.pending

fn supersession_event_expression(event: String) -> String {
  concat(concat("${{ github.event_name == '", event), "' }}")
}

fn cancel_in_progress_of_policy(policy: RunningSupersessionPolicy) -> CancelInProgressSpec {
  match policy {
    NeverSupersedeRunning => CancelInProgressBool { value: false }
    SupersedeRunningForEvent { event: e } =>
      CancelInProgressExpression { expression: supersession_event_expression(event: e) }
  }
}


// THE QUEUE AXIS EMITS FROM THE SAME ROW THE CLASSIFIER READS, or the policy is not the authority.
// Raised in review 58012: the running axis was already derived through cancel_in_progress_of_policy
// while the emitted mapping named ConcurrencyMappingQueueNotMax as a literal, so flipping the
// modeled policy to RetainPendingQueue would have moved the classifier and left CI in replacement
// mode -- one fact with two authorities, which is the thing DESIGN 3 forbids. Both axes now fold
// out of witness_floor_concurrency_policy, so the emission cannot disagree with the classifier.
//
// The QueueMax arm is not a hypothetical: GitHub forces cancel-in-progress into its own
// CancelInProgressWhenQueueMax vocabulary there, and this fold carries the running policy across
// that boundary rather than dropping it, so the retaining regime stays expressible without a
// second edit here.
fn queue_max_cancel_of_policy(policy: RunningSupersessionPolicy) -> CancelInProgressWhenQueueMax {
  match policy {
    NeverSupersedeRunning => QueueMaxCancelInProgressFalse
    SupersedeRunningForEvent { event: e } =>
      QueueMaxCancelInProgressExpression { expression: supersession_event_expression(event: e) }
  }
}

fn concurrency_of_policy(policy: WitnessFloorConcurrencyPolicy, group: String) -> ConcurrencySpec {
  let pending = policy.pending
  match pending {
    ReplaceExistingPending => ConcurrencyMappingQueueNotMax {
      group: group,
      cancel_in_progress: Present { value: cancel_in_progress_of_policy(policy: policy.running) },
      explicit_single: none
    }
    RetainPendingQueue => ConcurrencyMappingQueueMax {
      group: group,
      cancel_in_progress: Present { value: queue_max_cancel_of_policy(policy: policy.running) }
    }
  }
}

// The binaries the fold actually needs, and only those. `claim_executor` is the fold's entry;
// `gunbc` is the CLI the repository's own tooling invokes. The predecessor build job named
// nineteen binaries, most of which this cut deleted; naming a binary that no step runs buys
// nothing and costs a compile.
// `v1_src_dag_parse` was ADDED 2026-08-19 for a step that ran it, and REMOVED 2026-08-20 with that
// step. The consolidation folded the parse sweep into `--required-ci` as an in-process phase
// calling the same `cli_run` walk, so nothing in this job invokes the standalone binary any more
// -- and its row was breaking exactly the rule stated above.
//
// Caught in review of gunbc#8647, and the way it went wrong is worth the line: the consolidation
// witness ASSERTED `--bin v1_src_dag_parse` was present, using "CI builds the parse bin" as a
// proxy for "the parse sweep survives". A bad proxy -- survival is established by the composed run
// EXECUTING the phase (`parse OK 50 file(s) parse-clean`, run 32371293567), never by compiling a
// binary nothing runs -- so the witness was pinning the surplus in place as a requirement.
//
// The binary stays in the tree: running the parse sweep alone is the cheapest check available
// while editing src/v1, and it is a thin caller of the same function, not a second
// implementation. It is simply no longer CI's business.
// WHAT "FULL COMPILE" DOES AND DOES NOT MEAN, named because the 2026-08-25 split puts a job called
// `build` beside a phase called `v2-emission` and the pair invites one wrong inference. The v2
// emission phase compiles a `.dag` ENTRY TO RUST SOURCE and stops before cargo; the emit-compile
// phase beside it takes that source the rest of the way and cargo-builds it, over its own bounded
// entry roster and never over the corpus; this step cargo-builds exactly the two bins named below,
// because they are the two the jobs run. NEITHER COMPILES THE REST OF THE RUST WORKSPACE. A
// declared `[[bin]]` outside this list is not built by any required phase, so a hand-authored Rust
// bin can fall behind a generated struct and sit on main indefinitely -- not hypothetical:
// measured 2026-08-25, `infer_semantics_witness` fails to compile on main with six `E0063`s
// against `TypeEnv`, and nothing in the required run sees it.
//
// WIDENING THIS TO `--bins` IS NOT TAKEN HERE, for scope rather than cost: it changes what the
// required check COVERS, an operator decision, and would land red on a pre-existing defect this
// change did not cause. Named rather than absorbed so the gap stays countable -- DESIGN's
// 2026-08-25 row already declares the class, and this is a specimen escaping by a mode that row
// does not enumerate (the `.dag` side is clean and the drifted artifact is hand-authored Rust).
data witness_floor_required_bins: List<String> = ["claim_executor", "gunbc"]

// THE HEAL REVALIDATION INPUT. A heal that repairs a branch pushes a new head H1 with the Actions
// job credential, and H1 must be judged on its own tree rather than inherit H0's receipts, which
// describe a tree that no longer exists. tools.ci_heal_dispatch closes that by dispatching this
// workflow, and a dispatch that cannot say WHICH head it got would report a verdict about a head
// it cannot name: a fabricated revalidation, worse than the honest gap.
//
// CREATED-AND-HELD IS NOT NEVER-CREATED, AND THAT DISTINCTION IS AT TOP BILLING BECAUSE EVERY
// WORDING ERROR THIS CARRIER WENT THROUGH -- IN BOTH DIRECTIONS -- FOLLOWED FROM NOT HAVING THE TWO
// AS SEPARATE STATES. They have different remedies: a held run has a release path,
// POST /actions/runs/<id>/approve on THAT run, and a nonexistent one has none. A justification for
// the dispatch route that cannot say which state it is in cannot say what the dispatch buys.
//
// GITHUB WITHHOLDS EXECUTION, NOT CREATION, AND THE HOLD KEYS ON THE EVENT. A `pull_request` run IS
// created for the healed head and executes nothing; the dispatched run on the same head executes.
// So nothing judges H1 automatically by the ordinary route, and the dispatch is the DECLARED path
// because it is the only route to a healed head that runs without a human -- not because no run
// appears. THE MEASURED POPULATIONS LIVE ONCE, IN gunbc.rung_drop floor_cut_heal, whose subject is
// this actuation boundary; they are cited here and deliberately not restated, so one count has one
// authority.
//
// THE INSTRUMENT FAILURE IS KEPT AS A ROW, NOT DELETED WITH THE CONCLUSION IT PRODUCED, because it
// is the sharpest specimen in this repository of a proxy that fails toward the reading its reader
// wants. An earlier draft read this table and concluded that most such runs execute:
//
//   run           conclusion        created  -> run_started_at   attempt 1 jobs
//   33686753487   failure           21:44:15 -> 21:44:15         0
//   33705120607   cancelled         01:48:15 -> 01:58:31         0
//   33703032560   action_required   01:17:18 -> 03:04:23         0   (attempt 2 ran, 1h47m later)
//   33711005806   action_required   03:20:33 -> 03:20:33         0
//
// TWO PROXIES, BOTH BROKEN, BOTH IN THE SAME DIRECTION. `conclusion != action_required` cannot
// report "held", because a held run still concludes something -- `failure` and `cancelled` above.
// `created_at == run_started_at` cannot report "held" either, because equal timestamps are exactly
// what a run that never ran looks like. Neither proxy has a state in which it says "this executed
// nothing", so neither was ever evidence about executing. THE TEST THAT WOULD HAVE CAUGHT BOTH,
// and the one worth carrying past this subject: ask what input would make the instrument report the
// other way. Only ATTEMPT 1's JOB COUNT has that state, because zero is a value it can return.
// Read attempt 1, never the latest -- a human approving a held run leaves attempt 2 looking like an
// ordinary execution, which is what hid the class for 1h47m on 33703032560.
//
// SO THE BEHAVIOUR IS RELIABLE HOLDING, NOT INTERMITTENT FAILURE, and the carrier says the stronger
// simpler thing rather than the hedge the broken table once forced: 0 of 4 heal pushes started a
// job on attempt 1, and the single run that ever executed did so on attempt 2 after a release.
//
// THE ONE CONTROL THIS ROUTE RESTS ON. On sha 958f743f9e, identity and token are held CONSTANT --
// actor and triggering_actor are github-actions[bot] on both -- and only the EVENT varies: run
// 33711005806 (pull_request) executed nothing, while run 33711101968 (workflow_dispatch) started
// and bound that head in this very preflight.
//
// THE COST THE LOOSE PREMISE CONCEALED: the dispatched run is a SECOND run on that head, so each
// heal buys a whole additional required-CI run, and its check runs land beside the held run's on
// one commit, where a reader keyed by check NAME cannot tell them apart.
//
// BOUNDARY OBLIGATION, UNDETERMINED BECAUSE UNREADABLE FROM HERE RATHER THAN UNEXAMINED, AND IT
// DECIDES A CLAIM THIS CARRIER THEREFORE DOES NOT MAKE. Releasing a held run is
// POST /actions/runs/<id>/approve on THAT run, so whether a dispatched run's green clears the PR
// depends on which contexts branch protection requires -- and branches/main/protection answers 403
// `Resource not accessible by integration` to the credential these sessions carry, as does the
// Actions permissions surface. So: the dispatch closes the REVALIDATION gap, measured -- the healed
// head is bound and judged. It says NOTHING about the MERGE gate, and the held run is what
// protection was waiting on. Naming the 403 is the point: unreadable names its own remedy, a
// credential that can read those two surfaces; unexamined names nothing.
// So the input is the binding, not a label. The dispatch ref is necessarily a moving branch
// pointer (GitHub's endpoint accepts nothing else); this input carries the immutable subject
// beside it, and every ordinary lane binds BOTH its run subject (github.sha, which is what the
// check is attached to) and its own checkout against it before a witness can count. Absent, the
// run is an ordinary manual dispatch and claims no revalidation.
data ci_heal_expected_healed_sha_input_name: String = "expected_healed_sha"

data ci_heal_expected_healed_sha_input: DispatchInput = DispatchInput {
  name: ci_heal_expected_healed_sha_input_name,
  description: Present {
    value: "Immutable healed commit required by an auto-heal revalidation run"
  },
  required: false,
  default: none,
  type: InputString
}

// THE CHECKOUT SELECTS THE EXPECTED HEAD WHEN ONE IS NAMED, and the preflight below still compares
// -- the two are not redundant. Selecting the ref removes the window in which the branch advances
// between dispatch and checkout; comparing github.sha catches the window BEFORE that, in which the
// branch advanced before GitHub resolved the dispatch ref into the run subject. Selection alone
// would produce a run whose checkout is H1 and whose CHECK is attached to H2, which is exactly the
// silent wrong answer this input exists to make unwritable.
data ci_heal_checkout_ref_expression: String = "${{ inputs.expected_healed_sha || github.sha }}"

fn witness_floor_checkout_step() -> Step {
  UsesStep {
    name: Present { value: "Checkout" },
    id: none,
    uses: checkout_action,
    with: Present {
      value: [
        kv(key: "fetch-depth", value: yaml_int(n: 0)),
        kv(key: "ref", value: yaml_string(s: ci_heal_checkout_ref_expression))
      ]
    },
    env: none,
    if_condition: none,
    continue_on_error: none,
    timeout_minutes: none
  }
}

// CACHING IS DISABLED BECAUSE IT WAS DELETING OTHER JOBS' FILES MID-BUILD, a statement about this
// fleet rather than about the action.
//
// setup-rust-toolchain defaults `cache` to true, which runs Swatinem/rust-cache, which registers a
// POST step. Two of that step's cleanup functions are the deleters, confirmed against its source:
//
//   cleanBin(oldBins)    removes binaries from ~/.cargo/bin that were not in the baseline
//                        captured when THAT job started.
//   cleanRegistry(..)    removes directories from ~/.cargo/registry/src that do not match
//                        the CURRENT workspace's dependency set.
//
// Both are correct on a GitHub-hosted runner, where the home directory belongs to one job and dies
// with it. This fleet is self-hosted: /opt/actions-runner/<slot>/_work is per-slot while
// /home/ghrunner/.cargo is ONE directory shared by every slot on the host. So each job's baseline
// and dependency set are computed as though it owned the home, and its post step then deletes
// against a home other jobs are actively building in.
//
// The two observed symptoms are exactly the two functions. cleanBin gives `host tool unresolved:
// "cargo"` — cargo was present at 01:24:52 when the build step used it for 2m49s and absent later
// in the SAME job on the SAME PATH (run 32088114155), which no PATH misconfiguration can produce.
// cleanRegistry gives `ring-0.17.14/crypto/fipsmodule/ec/ecp_nistz.c: No such file or directory`
// during a build (run 32087371268, srv2-04), whose downstream noise is the `could not
// parse/generate dep info` class that reads like a target/ problem and is not.
//
// The selection effect corroborates it: the witness step is ~45 minutes and the build step ~3, so
// almost every hit lands in the witness step — what an overlap-probability argument predicts and a
// configuration error would not.
//
// WHY DISABLING RATHER THAN SPLITTING THE HOME. A per-slot CARGO_HOME also works and was the first
// candidate, but it multiplies the registry by the slot count on every host to solve a problem
// caused by a cache layer this fleet does not need: ~/.cargo already persists across jobs on a
// self-hosted runner, so restore-and-save buys nothing and only the cleanup half has an effect.
// Disabling is the smaller change and removes the mechanism outright.
//
// THE COST, STATED. Nothing now prunes ~/.cargo, so the registry grows without bound and becomes a
// disk-pressure question instead of a correctness one. The right trade — a full disk refuses
// loudly, a deleted source file mid-compile does not — but a real obligation, belonging to whoever
// owns fleet disk, not to this file.
// THIS STEP ASKS FOR rustfmt EXPLICITLY, for a measured incident rather than tidiness. Without the
// components key it installs a toolchain and takes whatever the runner's resolved default carries
// -- so the job passes on a runner whose default has rustfmt and fails on one whose default does
// not, with no relationship to the diff under test.
//
// Observed 2026-08-22 on gunbc#8860: `required-ci: FAILED PHASE regen refused: normalize
// candidate wt_b.rs: spawn rustfmt: No such file or directory (os error 2)`, while the witness
// floor in the same run passed 10130/10130 with failed=0. The regen phase writes the emitted
// artifact as a FIXED POINT of the formatter, so it must spawn rustfmt; nothing in this workflow
// had asked for it.
//
// THE OBVIOUS READING OF THAT ERROR IS WRONG AND THIS REPOSITORY ALREADY PAID TO LEARN IT.
// `gunbc.host_toolchain_components` `toolchain_completeness_authority_note` records the same error
// on the same host class 2026-07-25: rustfmt WAS present on the machine; the job principal had two
// rustup toolchains and defaulted to the one without it. A COMPONENT IS A PROPERTY OF A TOOLCHAIN,
// NOT OF A MACHINE, so "install rustfmt on that host" converges green while the gate keeps
// failing. The fix belongs in what this workflow ASKS FOR.
//
// `gunbc.fleet_workflow_steps` `ci_setup_rust_step` already passes this same key to this same
// action; this step was the one that did not.
fn witness_floor_toolchain_step() -> Step {
  UsesStep {
    name: Present { value: "Install Rust toolchain" },
    id: none,
    uses: setup_rust_action,
    with: Present {
      value: [
        kv(key: "components", value: yaml_string(s: "rustfmt")),
        kv(key: "cache", value: yaml_bool(b: false)),
      ]
    },
    env: none,
    if_condition: none,
    continue_on_error: none,
    timeout_minutes: none
  }
}

// One cargo invocation, no retry arm. The predecessor carried a fallback that re-ran the build
// with CARGO_BUILD_JOBS=1 and RUSTC_WRAPPER unset when the first attempt failed; that arm made
// an sccache deficit unobservable by construction (DESIGN section 5, the absorbing fallback), so
// it is not re-derived. A build failure is a build failure and stops the line.
fn witness_floor_build_script() -> String {
  repo_self_build_command(bins: witness_floor_required_bins)
}

// TWO INVOCATIONS, BECAUSE THEY BUILD DISJOINT PACKAGE SETS AND NEITHER SUBSUMES THE OTHER. The
// first is every declared bin target of the v1-compiler package. The second is the seven sibling
// packages of the derived stage0 partition, whose roster gunbc.repo_self_build reads from the
// partition authority itself. Before this pair the build lane covered only the first, which is how
// #9689 left main's whole-workspace build red on E0432 in v1-stage0-v1-infer while every required
// context stayed green -- the partition crates are the ONLY consumers that compile a stage0 module
// through a per-crate reexport closure, so a roster short of a module is invisible to the monolith
// and fatal to them.
//
// THE SECOND COMMAND IS THE EXECUTING FORM OF A WALL THAT HAS NO HERMETIC WITNESS. Its refusal is
// rustc's: a module missing from a roster reds E0432 naming the module and the referring module; a
// roster entry naming no file reds E0583 naming the entry. gunbc.repo_self_build carries why a
// joining witness is not available instead, and names the modeled Rust module dependency graph as
// the trigger for climbing off the compiler as oracle.
fn build_lane_build_script() -> String {
  join(
    [repo_self_build_all_bins_command(), repo_self_build_partition_crates_command()],
    "\n"
  )
}

data witness_floor_build_step_name: String = "Build the witness fold"

data heal_build_step_name: String = "Build the regenerator this job runs against its own checkout"

// THE STEP NAME IS A PARAMETER BECAUSE THE JOBS BUILD DIFFERENT THINGS FOR DIFFERENT REASONS.
// It was the literal "Build the witness fold" until the heal job bound this same function to
// build a regenerator, at which point one name would have described two obligations -- the
// meaning fork DESIGN section 3 names, and the emitted yaml is exactly where a reader looks to
// find out what a job does. The construction is shared; what it is FOR is per caller.
fn witness_floor_build_step(step_name: String, build_script: String) -> Step {
  RunStep {
    name: Present { value: step_name },
    id: Present { value: "build_witness_fold" },
    run: concat(build_script, "\n"),
    shell: none,
    env: none,
    working_directory: none,
    if_condition: none,
    continue_on_error: none,
    timeout_minutes: none
  }
}

// ONE INVOCATION OF OUR OWN BINARY PER JOB. Everything the run decides -- which witnesses exist,
// what identity each has, which frame it needs, and whether the roster is admissible at all -- is
// decided in .dag by v2.workflow.required_floor. This step passes the source roots and a LANE
// WORD, nothing else: no plan entry, plan function, batch id, worker role or selection flag,
// because the fold has no such concepts to configure.
//
// A LANE IS NOT A SELECTION. It names which JOB owns a phase, not which phases exist or which
// witnesses run; every phase is owned by exactly one lane and both lanes run on every push and
// pull request. Conflating the two turns a scheduling fact into a coverage claim, which is why the
// lane word is the only thing the transport carries.
//
// ONE INVOCATION PER LANE, AND THE ORDER WITHIN A LANE IS THE PROGRAM'S RATHER THAN THE JOB'S
// (operator directive, 2026-08-20: "i would much prefer if it was all handled within the
// witnesses step and within the gunbc binary, not at a github actions job level").
//
// **THAT DIRECTIVE IS NOW PARTLY SUPERSEDED, BY THE SAME OPERATOR, ON 2026-08-25** -- "we can add
// it as a parallel job in github actions - we can do the same for regen now, we have more
// runners". The half that SURVIVES is "within the gunbc binary": the phases still live there, each
// job names a lane rather than a phase list, and no step's precondition reads another phase's
// verdict, so the step-ladder defect below cannot return. The half SUPERSEDED is "not at a github
// actions job level", superseded by a fact about the transport rather than taste: PARALLELISM IS
// NOT EXPRESSIBLE IN THE BINARY. Two phases running concurrently means two runners, two checkouts
// and two toolchains, and one process on one runner can thread but cannot acquire a second
// machine. What the directive protected against -- SEQUENCING and PRECONDITIONS leaking into YAML
// -- is exactly what does not cross the new boundary: the two jobs carry no `needs` edge and no
// condition on each other, which is the whole content of running them in parallel.
//
// The paragraphs below describe a step ladder still gone and a consolidation that still holds
// WITHIN a lane; they are kept because what they establish -- why a YAML step list is the wrong
// place for a phase order -- is unchanged by the split.
//
// WHAT THIS REPLACES: four steps -- parse, regen, regen-fixed-point, floor -- each its own process,
// their ORDER a YAML list, each precondition an `if:` naming another step's `outcome`. The
// fixed-point step additionally received pass 1's digest by READING THE RECEIPT FILE the regen
// process had just written -- the tell that a process boundary sat where a function call belonged:
// `run_required_regen_fixed_point` has taken `pass1_digest: Option<String>` all along and CI
// passed it `None`.
//
// `--required-ci` runs the required phases in one process and reports the complete ledger: the
// phases are independent, so an earlier failure no longer skips a later phase and one defect can
// no longer hide the others. The line still stops -- nonzero exit on any failed phase -- and a
// phase that could not run reports SKIPPED as its own state, never as silence or a pass.
//
// THE ROSTER IS READ FROM THE RUN, NOT FROM THIS COMMENT. Each lane announces every phase -- one
// `phase <name>` line for the phases it owns and one `ROUTED to lane <other>` line for the phases
// it does not -- so one job's log names the whole roster. The paragraph below records the
// 2026-08-21 cut to three; #9035 then added v2-emission, and the 2026-08-25 split partitioned the
// roster into `build` (regen, v2-emission, partition-crates, emit-compile) and `witnesses` (parse,
// floor). A count transcribed here has already gone stale twice. The cut phases that paragraph
// enumerates stay cut -- neither the addition nor the split restored any of them.
//
// THE ROSTER WAS THREE PHASES, NOT FOUR (operator ruling, 2026-08-21): the src/v1 .dag parse
// sweep, the regen first-generation comparison, and the witness floor. The fixed point left the
// required mode with the four other cut phases, and the in-memory digest handoff that justified
// collapsing it went with it -- so the paragraph above describes the step ladder this replaced,
// and the digest sentence is history rather than a live mechanism. The cut capabilities keep their
// standalone entry points (`--required-regen-fixed-point`, `--behavioral-receipt-*`); what ended
// is their enrolment.
//
// THE FLOOR STEP'S COMMAND IS NOT BUILT HERE. It is `gunbc.fabric_witness_run`
// `floor_run_command`, the same ArgvCommand the fabric authorizes, rendered for this transport by
// `extdeps.exec.command` `shell_command_render`. An earlier revision built the invocation a SECOND
// time -- its own binary path, its own --required-floor, its own --source-root spelling, folded
// over the same witness_layer_roots -- and #8576 pinned the two together with a witness asserting
// they agreed on source roots. That was validation standing where construction was available
// (DESIGN §5): it stayed green while the binary path, the flag spelling or the argument ORDER
// drifted, because agreeing on the roots is not agreeing on the command.
//
// WHY THE PATHS ARE RELATIVE AND cd DOES THE WORK. shell_command_render single-quotes every word,
// citing IEEE 1003.1-2017 §2.2.2 -- correct for an argv and fatal for a word carrying a shell
// variable, since '$ROOT/dag' does not expand. So $ROOT is not IN the argv: the script cds to the
// root first and the argv stays genuinely an argv, which is what lets a modeled renderer quote it
// at all. The v1-parse step already establishes `cd "$ROOT"` here. Same binary, same flags, same
// roots, resolved against the same directory.
//
// witness_floor_source_root_flags IS DELETED HERE, and this clause previously said the opposite:
// that the helper survives because the two regen scripts below build --required-regen and
// --required-regen-fixed-point as separate commands, so its dissolution was someone else's. True
// when written; #8647 falsified it: the consolidation folded all four phases into one
// --required-ci process, deleting both regen scripts, so when this branch merged main the helper
// had exactly one consumer left -- the floor script -- and rendering that from floor_run_command
// removes it. Recorded rather than silently dropped, because a paragraph that once justified
// keeping something is evidence a reader will reuse, and the justification expired before the
// thing did.
fn required_lane_run_script(command: ArgvCommand) -> String {
  concat(
    concat("ROOT=", concat(ci_repo_root_shell(), "\n")),
    concat(
      "cd \"$ROOT\"\n",
      concat(shell_command_render(command: command), "\n")
    )
  )
}

fn witness_floor_run_script() -> String {
  required_lane_run_script(command: witnesses_lane_measurement_command(measurement_receipt: required_ci_measurement_receipt_path))
}

fn build_lane_run_script() -> String {
  required_lane_run_script(command: build_lane_run_command())
}

// THE FLOOR RUNS LAST AND THEREFORE DECLARES ITS PRECONDITION TOO (operator directive, 2026-08-20:
// "move regen before witnesses"). Regen costs ~4 minutes and the floor ~30, so ordering the cheap
// total check first turns a drift report from a 35-minute wait into a 5-minute one -- the ordinary
// cheap-refusal-first argument, and why the directive is right.
//
// But the reorder is not a pure move, and shipping it as one would have re-created the exact
// defect the two regen steps were added to fix, pointing the other way. A step with no `if`
// inherits GitHub's default `success()` -- the conjunction of every EARLIER step -- so once regen
// sits ahead of the floor, an unconditioned floor silently acquires regen's verdict as a
// precondition. A regen red would DISARM the whole witness floor, and 9,008 witnesses would report
// `skipped` because a stale stage0 mirror -- a materially different fact with nothing to say about
// whether the corpus's claims hold. That is the empty-observation narrow again -- `the floor did
// not fail` standing in for `the floor was never evaluated` -- and strictly worse than the
// original, because the population silenced is three orders of magnitude larger.
//
// So the floor's real precondition is stated for the same reason regen's is, and it is the same
// one: the binary exists. The three claims -- floor, regen, regen determinism -- are mutually
// independent except where regen's receipt genuinely feeds the fixed-point step, and after this
// change every step declares what it actually needs rather than inheriting its position. Order is
// free to change again without silently rewiring any step's condition, which is what made the
// original ordering a hazard rather than a preference.
//
// MEASURED CORRECTION, run 32323690924. The paragraph above was right about the hazard and wrong
// about the remedy, and CI refused the remedy. On that run the build succeeded, so
// `steps.build_witness_fold.outcome == 'success'` evaluated TRUE -- and the floor reported
// `skipped` anyway, behind a regen failure. Declaring an `if` does not, on its own, displace the
// implicit `success()`: GitHub substitutes the author's expression for the default ONLY when it
// contains a status function (`success()`, `failure()`, `always()`, `cancelled()`). A bare
// `steps.*.outcome` comparison is not one, so the default is silently CONJOINED rather than
// replaced, and every condition below still read `success() && <declared>`. The declaration was
// decorative: the floor remained wired to regen's verdict, and the 9,008-witness silencing above
// happened on the first run that exercised it.
//
// So each condition carries `!cancelled()` -- a status function, which actually performs the
// substitution -- rather than `always()`, because a cancelled run should stop, and only a FAILED
// earlier step must not propagate. The discriminator worth keeping: a precondition that reads
// correctly is not evidence that it binds. The tell was a step skipping while its own stated
// condition was true, visible only by reading the step's outcome beside the condition's operands,
// never from the workflow text.
fn witness_step_status_guard() -> String {
  concat(witness_step_status_guard_expression(), " && ")
}

// The status function itself, without the conjunction tail. The job-level condition needs it
// bare; the step-level prefix above needs it with `&& `. One spelling, two shapes -- a second
// literal would be the fork that drifts the moment the guard is reconsidered.
fn witness_step_status_guard_expression() -> String {
  "!cancelled()"
}

fn witness_floor_precondition() -> String {
  concat(witness_step_status_guard(), "steps.build_witness_fold.outcome == 'success'")
}

// AN INSTRUMENT, NOT A CHECK. It records and it never refuses.
//
// `rustc` and `rustfmt` have both vanished after successful use in the same job. The syscall
// message reports absence but neither when nor why the file disappeared, so it cannot distinguish
// disk pressure, age-based cleanup and another runner's initialization. A PATH/toolchain listing
// added after the build answered a different question and was silent when setup itself failed.
//
// EVERY PROBE IS TOTAL BY CONSTRUCTION -- fallbacks and output-consuming pipelines turn absent
// files, commands and filesystems into prefixed readings rather than nonzero exits, so the step
// cannot fail and needs no `continue-on-error`. The distinction is not cosmetic:
// `continue-on-error` is a step that MAY report a verdict and has been told to be ignored, the
// escape-hatch shape DESIGN 5 forbids. A step with no verdict to give is a different thing.
// THIS INSTRUMENT IS TEMPORARY. Its readings distinguish the three live eviction hypotheses:
// falling free blocks/inodes identifies pressure reclamation; old mtimes plus wall-clock/uptime
// identify age or periodic cleanup; and an end disappearance coincident with another job's start
// on the same host/slot identifies runner-initiated cleanup. The start observation is deliberately
// the FIRST step, before toolchain_home_isolation_step legitimately removes the previous job's
// homes. The end observation is deliberately the LAST step and uses `always()`: the failing runs
// are the subject, so a success-conditioned end observation would erase the evidence it exists to
// collect. An empty start population is still evidence: setup can fail before creating a new
// toolchain, and the first probe records what the previous job left before isolation deletes it.
// Every output line has one prefix and a phase so logs can be joined across jobs/runs.
//
// DIRECTORY MTIME IS THE EVENT-TIME PROXY. Binary state at two boundaries leaves a deletion
// window as wide as the job; unlink updates the containing directory's mtime, narrowing the join
// against another job start or a periodic cleanup boundary to seconds without a background
// sampler. Both present and absent parents are rendered, so removal of the whole tree cannot look
// like a probe that failed to run.
//
// Shell remains a §6 tell, bounded by the explicit condition below rather than permanent workflow
// furniture. Runner microVM isolation is independently sufficient because it makes another job's
// cleanup incapable of reaching this filesystem even before the particular deleter is known.
data toolchain_filesystem_probe_dissolution_condition: DissolutionCondition = unbound_dissolution(description: "dissolve-on: toolchain_filesystem_probe -- delete the start/end runner-filesystem instrument after its joined readings identify and the fleet fixes the toolchain deleter, OR after per-job runner microVMs make the shared filesystem eviction class impossible")

fn witness_toolchain_filesystem_probe_script(phase: String) -> String {
  concat(
    concat("# ", concat(dissolution_description(condition: toolchain_filesystem_probe_dissolution_condition), "\n")),
    concat(
      concat("GUNBC_TOOLCHAIN_FS_PREFIX='GUNBC_TOOLCHAIN_FS phase=", concat(phase, "'\n")),
      concat(
        "echo \"$GUNBC_TOOLCHAIN_FS_PREFIX time_utc=$(date -u +%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ 2>/dev/null || echo unavailable) host=$(hostname 2>/dev/null || echo unavailable) runner=$\{RUNNER_NAME:-unset}\"\n",
        concat(
          "echo \"$GUNBC_TOOLCHAIN_FS_PREFIX runner_temp=$\{RUNNER_TEMP:-unset} rustup_home=$\{RUSTUP_HOME:-unset} cargo_home=$\{CARGO_HOME:-unset} uptime_seconds=$(cut -d' ' -f1 /proc/uptime 2>/dev/null || echo unavailable)\"\n",
          concat(
            "echo \"$GUNBC_TOOLCHAIN_FS_PREFIX loadavg=$(tr ' ' ',' < /proc/loadavg 2>/dev/null || echo unavailable)\"\n",
            concat(
              "df -Pk \"$\{RUNNER_TEMP:-/path-that-does-not-exist}\" 2>&1 | while IFS= read -r GUNBC_TOOLCHAIN_FS_LINE; do echo \"$GUNBC_TOOLCHAIN_FS_PREFIX df_blocks=$GUNBC_TOOLCHAIN_FS_LINE\"; done\n",
              concat(
                "df -Pik \"$\{RUNNER_TEMP:-/path-that-does-not-exist}\" 2>&1 | while IFS= read -r GUNBC_TOOLCHAIN_FS_LINE; do echo \"$GUNBC_TOOLCHAIN_FS_PREFIX df_inodes=$GUNBC_TOOLCHAIN_FS_LINE\"; done\n",
                concat(
                  "for GUNBC_TOOLCHAIN_FS_PATH in \"$\{RUNNER_TEMP:-/path-that-does-not-exist}\"/rustup/toolchains/*/bin/rustc \"$\{RUNNER_TEMP:-/path-that-does-not-exist}\"/rustup/toolchains/*/bin/rustfmt \"$\{RUNNER_TEMP:-/path-that-does-not-exist}\"/cargo/bin/cargo; do if [ -e \"$GUNBC_TOOLCHAIN_FS_PATH\" ]; then GUNBC_TOOLCHAIN_FS_STAT=$(stat -c 'size=%s mtime_epoch=%Y mtime=%y inode=%i' \"$GUNBC_TOOLCHAIN_FS_PATH\" 2>&1 || echo stat-unavailable); echo \"$GUNBC_TOOLCHAIN_FS_PREFIX kind=file path=$GUNBC_TOOLCHAIN_FS_PATH exists=yes $GUNBC_TOOLCHAIN_FS_STAT\"; else echo \"$GUNBC_TOOLCHAIN_FS_PREFIX kind=file path=$GUNBC_TOOLCHAIN_FS_PATH exists=no size=unavailable mtime_epoch=unavailable mtime=unavailable inode=unavailable\"; fi; done\n",
                  "for GUNBC_TOOLCHAIN_FS_PATH in \"$\{RUNNER_TEMP:-/path-that-does-not-exist}\"/rustup/toolchains/*/bin \"$\{RUNNER_TEMP:-/path-that-does-not-exist}\"/rustup/toolchains \"$\{RUNNER_TEMP:-/path-that-does-not-exist}\"/cargo/bin; do if [ -d \"$GUNBC_TOOLCHAIN_FS_PATH\" ]; then GUNBC_TOOLCHAIN_FS_STAT=$(stat -c 'mtime_epoch=%Y mtime=%y inode=%i' \"$GUNBC_TOOLCHAIN_FS_PATH\" 2>&1 || echo stat-unavailable); echo \"$GUNBC_TOOLCHAIN_FS_PREFIX kind=directory path=$GUNBC_TOOLCHAIN_FS_PATH exists=yes $GUNBC_TOOLCHAIN_FS_STAT\"; else echo \"$GUNBC_TOOLCHAIN_FS_PREFIX kind=directory path=$GUNBC_TOOLCHAIN_FS_PATH exists=no mtime_epoch=unavailable mtime=unavailable inode=unavailable\"; fi; done\n"
                )
              )
            )
          )
        )
      )
    )
  )
}

fn witness_toolchain_filesystem_probe_step(phase: String, condition: String) -> Step {
  RunStep {
    name: Present { value: concat("Record runner filesystem at job ", phase) },
    id: none,
    run: witness_toolchain_filesystem_probe_script(phase: phase),
    shell: none,
    env: none,
    working_directory: none,
    if_condition: Present { value: condition },
    continue_on_error: none,
    timeout_minutes: none
  }
}

fn witness_toolchain_filesystem_start_probe_step() -> Step {
  witness_toolchain_filesystem_probe_step(phase: "start", condition: "always()")
}

fn witness_toolchain_filesystem_end_probe_step() -> Step {
  witness_toolchain_filesystem_probe_step(phase: "end", condition: "always()")
}

// A WALL, NOT PART OF THE INSTRUMENT. The filesystem probe above must be total because it reports
// evidence at a boundary; this step must refuse because an unset isolated home means the job can
// share a mutable toolchain with another runner slot. Keeping the decision separate from the
// recorder prevents either fact from weakening the other: a missing stat cannot trip this wall,
// and a missing home cannot be rendered as a merely observational `unset` and allowed onward.
fn witness_toolchain_homes_isolated_guard_step() -> Step {
  RunStep {
    name: Present { value: "Require isolated toolchain homes" },
    id: none,
    run: concat(ci_toolchain_homes_isolated_or_refuse_command, "\n"),
    shell: none,
    env: none,
    working_directory: none,
    if_condition: Present { value: "!cancelled()" },
    continue_on_error: none,
    timeout_minutes: none
  }
}

// THE BUILD LANE'S RUN STEP. Same binary, same mode, same source roots, one word different --
// which is the whole point of the lane being a parameter rather than a second invocation.
//
// THE STEP NAME DOES NOT ENUMERATE THE PHASES, A CORRECTION RATHER THAN VAGUENESS. It read
// "(regen, v2 emission)" while the lane carried four: a label listing a roster is a TRANSCRIPTION
// of something the run itself prints, and it drifts silently every time the roster grows, which it
// has done three times in a week. The cost is real: a session triaging a red step narrowed to the
// two phases the label named and could not reach the third, which was the one that failed.
// DESIGN's standing rule is to name the instrument and never transcribe its output; the instrument
// here is the required mode itself, which announces one `phase <name>` line per phase the lane
// owns before any of them runs. Read the roster there.
fn build_lane_run_step() -> Step {
  RunStep {
    name: Present { value: "Required CI: build lane (phases named by the run, not by this label)" },
    id: none,
    run: build_lane_run_script(),
    shell: none,
    env: none,
    working_directory: none,
    if_condition: Present { value: witness_floor_precondition() },
    continue_on_error: none,
    timeout_minutes: none
  }
}

fn witness_floor_run_step() -> Step {
  RunStep {
    name: Present { value: "D0-MEASURE: witnesses lane" },
    id: Present { value: "required_ci_measure" },
    run: witness_floor_run_script(),
    shell: none,
    env: none,
    working_directory: none,
    if_condition: Present { value: witness_floor_precondition() },
    continue_on_error: Present { value: true },
    timeout_minutes: none
  }
}

data required_ci_measurement_receipt_path: String = "required_ci_measurement_receipt.json"
data required_ci_measurement_receipt_artifact_name: String = "required-ci-measurement-receipt"
data required_ci_measurement_build_outcome_expression: String = "${{ steps.build_witness_fold.outcome }}"

fn required_ci_measurement_build_outcome_env() -> List<YamlKeyValue> {
  [kv(
    key: required_ci_measurement_build_outcome_var,
    value: yaml_string(s: required_ci_measurement_build_outcome_expression)
  )]
}

fn required_ci_measurement_finalize_script_outcome() -> String? {
  match bash_fold_serialize_program(
    stmts: required_ci_measurement_finalize_stmts(receipt_path: required_ci_measurement_receipt_path),
    set_e: false
  ) {
    Accepted { value: script, diagnostics: _ } => Present { value: script }
    Rejected { diagnostics: _ } => none
  }
}

fn required_ci_measurement_finalize_script() -> String {
  match required_ci_measurement_finalize_script_outcome() {
    Present { value: script } => script
    Absent => ""
  }
}

fn required_ci_measurement_adjudicate_script_outcome() -> String? {
  match bash_fold_serialize_program(
    stmts: required_ci_measurement_adjudicate_stmts(receipt_path: required_ci_measurement_receipt_path),
    set_e: false
  ) {
    Accepted { value: script, diagnostics: _ } => Present { value: script }
    Rejected { diagnostics: _ } => none
  }
}

fn required_ci_measurement_scripts_are_renderable() -> Bool {
  match required_ci_measurement_finalize_script_outcome() {
    Present { value: _ } => match required_ci_measurement_adjudicate_script_outcome() {
      Present { value: _ } => true
      Absent => false
    }
    Absent => false
  }
}

fn required_ci_measurement_finalize_step() -> Step {
  RunStep {
    name: Present { value: "D0-MEASURE: seal an unreached receipt when the instrument did not return" },
    id: none,
    run: required_ci_measurement_finalize_script(), shell: none,
    env: Present { value: required_ci_measurement_build_outcome_env() }, working_directory: none,
    if_condition: Present { value: "always() && !cancelled()" }, continue_on_error: none, timeout_minutes: none
  }
}

fn required_ci_measurement_adjudicate_step() -> Step {
  RunStep {
    name: Present { value: "D0-ADJUDICATE: consume the published measurement receipt" },
    id: none,
    run: match required_ci_measurement_adjudicate_script_outcome() {
      Present { value: script } => script
      Absent => ""
    },
    shell: none, env: Present { value: required_ci_measurement_build_outcome_env() }, working_directory: none,
    if_condition: Present { value: "always() && !cancelled()" }, continue_on_error: none, timeout_minutes: none
  }
}

fn required_ci_measurement_publish_condition() -> String {
  "always() && !cancelled()"
}

// A DECLARED PROVISIONAL BOUND, NOT A DERIVED ONE, AND THE DIFFERENCE IS THE WHOLE POINT.
//
// Absent, this job inherits GitHub's 360-minute default -- never unbounded, but nobody's decision
// and longer than any observed run needs. The cost is not wall clock: a self-hosted runner is held
// for the duration, and the step's log is UNREADABLE until the job ends, so an over-long ceiling
// converts a failed run into hours of zero information. A bounded failure is strictly more
// informative than an unbounded wait.
//
// 180 is NOT derived from a completed run, because no run of this fold has ever completed;
// deriving it would require the completion time this bound exists to force. It is half the
// inherited default, above every phase sequence measured so far (preparation 553s plus
// published-mock projection 47s locally, at bfecfd961c1), and low enough that the log arrives the
// same working hour as the failure. Calling it derived would be the fabricated plausible output
// DESIGN section 5 forbids, one level up -- a number with no measurement behind it wearing a
// measurement's authority.
//
// The two regen steps added 2026-08-19 are covered by the SAME job-level bound, provisionally, for
// the same reason: neither has completed in CI either, so there is nothing to derive a separate
// figure from. A widening of what the existing provisional number covers, not a second number --
// DESIGN §2 (one concept, one authority) applies to a timeout bound as much as to any other fact.
//
// RESTORATION TRIGGER: the first run that completes this fold, floor and regen alike. Real wall
// times replace this constant, and this note dissolves with the provisional bound it explains.
// ONE LIST, TWO PROJECTIONS. The steps GitHub sees and the capability roles the closure reads are
// derived from the same rows, so they cannot drift apart. A second hand-maintained list of roles
// beside the step list would be the §3 fork this closure exists to close, reintroduced as its own
// fix.
type WitnessFloorBoundStep {
  step: Step
  role: StepCapabilityRole
  step_name: String
}

// EACH PATH IS ONE ROW BECAUSE TWO CONSUMERS NOW READ IT. The writer learns the filename from the
// environment variable this job sets; the matching upload step names the same file to collect.
// Spelled twice -- six literals across the three files -- each pair could drift silently in the
// worst direction: the uploader looks for a file the writer never wrote, `if-no-files-found:
// error` fires, and the failure reads as "the floor produced no roster" when the truth is "the
// writer and the uploader disagree about a filename". One row per file makes that disagreement
// unrepresentable rather than caught.
data required_floor_disposition_path: String = "required_floor_disposition.tsv"

data required_floor_disposition_artifact_name: String = "required-floor-disposition"

data expected_red_roster_join_path: String = "expected_red_roster_join.tsv"

data expected_red_roster_join_artifact_name: String = "expected-red-roster-join"

data long_home_storage_agreement_path: String = "long_home_storage_agreement.tsv"

data long_home_storage_agreement_artifact_name: String = "long-home-storage-agreement"

// THE FOURTH FILE, AND THE ONLY ONE THAT WAS NEVER EVEN WRITTEN.
//
// The three rows above record files the floor wrote on every run and nobody collected. This one is
// a rung below: `cli_run.rs` reads GUNBC_REQUIRED_FLOOR_CLAIM_COST and calls
// `write_required_floor_claim_cost_tsv` -- one row per EXECUTED claim, carrying identity, module,
// outcome, verdict_reached, BOTH clocks (wall_ms and cpu_ms) and the evaluator-step work measure
// (eval_steps, marginal of stored shared fills, the one column that is not a clock and therefore
// the one column invariant across the execution envelope) -- but this job never set the
// variable, so the writer was never armed and the population was recomputed and discarded on every
// required run since the floor cut.
//
// WHAT THAT COSTS: a measurement the corpus could not take. The floor carries TWO INDEPENDENT
// BLOCKING DEADLINES on two different clocks: `v2.workflow.required_floor`
// `required_floor_claim_cpu_safety_limit_ms` and `required_floor_claim_wall_safety_limit_ms` --
// NEITHER VALUE IS TRANSCRIBED HERE, because this clause carried a parenthesised value beside each
// name and the wall one was wrong: it disagreed with the constant set by the very change that
// wrote the note, and with the preemption fixture one file away. Two numerals, one authority, and
// the copy rotted inside its own diff.
//
// THE FIRST REPAIR OF THIS CLAUSE RE-TRANSCRIBED THE VALUES WHILE EXPLAINING WHY NOT TO, which is
// why no figure appears above: writing "the wall limit is set to N" to document the rot recreates
// the coupling one sentence later. A rot receipt names the constant; a reader who needs a value
// reads the function. The two are deliberately different so a hermetic in-process claim
// descheduled by a co-tenant host does not read as a runaway. The only per-claim two-clock output
// any run emits is the `[over-cost]` log line -- and that population is selected by
// `required_floor_cost_basis`, which is the constant `CpuCost`: a row is printed because its CPU
// exceeded the diagnostic cost line, and its wall column is carried along.
//
// So the CPU margin distribution is derivable from run output and the WALL margin distribution IS
// NOT, at any sample size: a claim with low CPU and high wall -- exactly the blocked-or-descheduled
// failure the wall limit exists to catch -- is never printed. Measured on the five main runs pinned
// in the PR body (33036922596, 33037011305, 33037689214, 33037870402, 33037904520; 690 pooled
// rows): wall minus CPU is at most 15 ms on every printed row, mean 6.7 ms. That is not evidence
// that no wall-bound claim exists; it is the selection effect stating this sample cannot contain
// one. Reading a wall distribution off it would be a population selected on one clock reported
// against the other clock's limit.
//
// So this is not a convenience artifact: it is the only route to the wall limit's margin
// population, and arming it changes no threshold and no verdict --
// `write_required_floor_claim_cost_tsv` is a pure write of rows the fold already produced.
//
// `if-no-files-found: error` IS SAFE HERE ON THE SAME GROUNDS AS THE THREE ABOVE, checked the same
// way rather than inherited: the write is guarded only on its own path being set, which this job
// now sets, and it is reached on the same fold-completion path as the disposition and long-home
// writes -- strictly EARLIER in that sequence, so any refusal returning before those three also
// returns before this one. It is never absent on a run that reached its writes.
data required_floor_claim_cost_path: String = "required_floor_claim_cost.tsv"

data required_floor_claim_cost_artifact_name: String = "required-floor-claim-cost"

// THE FIFTH FILE: THE CROSS-CLAIM DEMAND CENSUS, and it answers a question the four above
// structurally cannot.
//
// The per-claim cost receipt says WHAT each claim was charged. It cannot say what the charge was
// FOR, and on this corpus most of a tail row's charge is not its own work: measured on main run
// 33615659836, a witness and its discriminating RED -- which do materially different work -- cost
// within 2ms of each other while both sit near the ceiling, and one 34-row module spans 0ms to the
// ceiling in three bands that track WHICH SHARED PRODUCER a row forces rather than what it
// asserts. That is the floor's fresh-frame-per-claim boundary re-deriving each closure's pure
// substrate once per claim, and DESIGN section 2 prices it as authored duplication whose least
// common ancestor is the RUN.
//
// THE REPAIR ALREADY EXISTS AND ITS DISCOVERY MECHANISM DID NOT. `v2.workflow.floor_pure_producer_share`
// serves one evaluation of a declared pure producer across claim frames -- and its roster is
// hand-authored, because the interpreter's recompute ledger is scoped to ONE evaluation frame and
// reports only keys re-hit inside it. A producer evaluated exactly once per claim, in thousands of
// claims, has count=1 in every frame's ledger and appears in none of them. So the instrument that
// ranks redundant recompute was blind to the entire population the roster is enrolled from, and
// candidates were discovered when a budget refusal landed on an unrelated lane's pull request.
// `absorb_claim_recompute_demand` folds each claim's ledger into a run-scoped census before the
// frame is dropped, and this artifact is that census.
//
// WHAT IT DOES NOT ANSWER, said here because an artifact is read for more than it measures. It
// explains the LEVEL -- why a family sits pinned near the ceiling -- and not the VARIANCE: a
// per-closure constant is the same on two runs of one tree, while 32 rows have been measured with
// byte-identical eval_steps and cpu up 1.31-1.80x across runs over a byte-identical payload. The
// two compose (the constant puts the family AT the line, something run-to-run decides which rows
// cross). Two receipts, neither a cross-run comparison: two rows of one module measured 502ms and
// EXACTLY 500ms against the 500ms budget, and one row was observed crossing the two populations on
// ONE tree -- run 33620893203 attempt 1 records it INTERRUPTED-BEFORE-VERDICT with cost=UNMEASURED
// while attempt 2 of the same run records it COMPLETED-OVER-COST-REQUIREMENT at cpu_ms=500. Which
// population a row lands in at that margin is decided by whether the poll fired before or after
// the work finished. The pair is a BOUND beside a VALUE rather than two measurements of one
// quantity, so no cost is written for attempt 1: an interrupted row's reported figure names where
// the poll observed the ceiling, which is a property of the budget. Both citations pin the ATTEMPT, because a bare run id and a bare job id
// resolve to the latest one and a rerun silently changes what they serve. The tipping
// variable is separately open. And a cost inside a native builtin called directly from a claim
// body is not a row here at all, because the ledger keys pure named fn evaluations -- the same
// boundary std.evaluation_budget's opaque-host-call note draws for the deadline.
//
// THE COST COLUMN IS NOT ADDITIVE AND THE ARTIFACT SAYS SO IN ITS OWN SUMMARY LINE. Durations are
// inclusive of callees, so a producer and everything it calls both appear and their figures
// overlap; summing the column counts the same nanoseconds once per level of nesting. On the first
// run that produced this artifact the naive sum over shared rows was ~1850s against a run whose
// entire claim-side CPU was 130s. So the summary carries claim_cpu_total_ms as the ceiling any
// true total must sit under, and a displaceable-cost figure needs self-time, which the ledger does
// not carry yet.
//
// THE NEGATIVE CONTROL IS EXECUTED RATHER THAN PROMISED, and it is the strongest thing that can
// be said about this artifact's separation of discovery from policy. The two rust target models
// were enrolled in the share roster and REMOVED, because a measured present-versus-absent
// experiment priced their serve above their recompute. They are exactly the kind of row this
// census ranks highly -- and on the first run that produced the artifact, the rust target-model
// chain does rank in the top ten by cross-claim recomputation while remaining unenrolled and
// unenrollable by any path from here. Demand is observed; enrolment stays a separate decision
// with its own measurement, in this order: demand observed, candidate selected, present/absent
// serve experiment on ONE exact tree, portability and argument-reuse evidence, serve cheaper than
// recompute, and only then a roster edit in floor_pure_producer_share.
//
// IT GATES NOTHING AND ENROLS NOTHING. A row is a CANDIDATE whose SERVE cost is unmeasured, and
// `floor_pure_producer_share` records the case where the serve lost to the recompute and the row
// was removed. Arming this variable changes no threshold and no verdict: the writer is a pure
// write of rows the fold already produced, on the same completion path as the four above.
data required_floor_cross_claim_demand_path: String = "required_floor_cross_claim_demand.tsv"

data required_floor_cross_claim_demand_artifact_name: String = "required-floor-cross-claim-demand"

// THE FLOOR HAS BEEN WRITING THREE FILES ON EVERY RUN AND THROWING ALL THREE AWAY.
//
// cli_run.rs reads GUNBC_REQUIRED_FLOOR_DISPOSITION, GUNBC_EXPECTED_RED_ROSTER_JOIN and
// GUNBC_LONG_HOME_STORAGE_AGREEMENT, and `write_required_floor_disposition_tsv`,
// `write_expected_red_roster_join_tsv` and `write_long_home_storage_agreement_tsv` emit them. The
// disposition file carries one row per OFFERED site: identity, disposition, and the matched prefix
// where one applies. All three variables are already set on this job. Nothing ever collected the
// files, so every run tore its runner down and took them.
//
// This is ONE unwired last hop with three instances, so it is fixed at the class, by one step
// shape applied three times. Fixing one site would leave the other two for someone to rediscover
// at the cost of the whole investigation.
//
// WHAT THAT COST, concretely: gunbc#8860 added three witnesses and could not show they were
// discovered. The `planned=` delta looked like proof and is not -- discovery is deterministic per
// commit but confounded, one unrelated line has been measured moving `offered` by 14 -- and the
// log carries no per-identity output for a passing witness. So the run answered neither "did my
// witness run" nor "was it declined, and why", while holding the file that answers the second.
//
// WHAT THIS DOES NOT ANSWER, and the PR body says it too: a row reading Planned means the identity
// was DISCOVERED AND ROUTED. It does not mean the claim executed, still less that it passed --
// `ClaimOutcome::Pass` increments a counter and discards the identity, so no execution roster
// exists anywhere to upload. This narrows the question; it does not close it.
//
// `if-no-files-found: error` IS SAFE ON ALL THREE, checked rather than assumed -- `error` on a file
// the run does not always write would redden green runs. cli_run.rs guards the join file on
// `roster_join_active`, which is `GUNBC_EXPECTED_RED_ROSTER_JOIN.is_some()`, and this job sets it;
// the other two are guarded only on their own paths being set, likewise set here. So all three are
// written whenever the floor reaches its writes. The one way a file is absent is a refusal that
// returns before them -- a run whose line has already stopped, where a second red is noise rather
// than a fabricated verdict.
//
// `if-no-files-found: error` IS ALSO LOAD-BEARING ON ALL THREE. The default is `warn`: it would
// upload nothing, go green, and hand a reader an empty artifact in which "no sites were declined"
// and "the file was never written" are indistinguishable. A blank is not evidence.
// `gunbc.fleet_workflow_steps` `ci_release_bins_upload_step` already sets it.
//
// THE CONDITION UPLOADS ALL THREE AFTER A FAILED FLOOR TOO, the case that matters most: a failed
// run is exactly when someone wants to know what was admitted. It reuses
// witness_floor_precondition -- not cancelled, and the fold was built -- rather than the action's
// success-only default, so a red floor still publishes all three files.
// WHY THREE STEPS AND NOT ONE BUNDLED STEP WITH THREE PATHS, MEASURED RATHER THAN PREFERRED.
// gunbc#8875 landed the bundled form: one artifact, three newline-separated paths,
// `if-no-files-found: warn`. Flipping that flag to `error` does not produce this step's guarantee,
// and the difference is in the action: actions/upload-artifact resolves all path patterns through
// ONE globber into ONE combined file list, and evaluates `if-no-files-found` on
// `searchResult.filesToUpload.length === 0` -- the whole list, once. So under the bundled shape,
// with EITHER flag value, two files present and one missing uploads the two and says nothing: the
// absence this evidence exists to make visible is exactly the absence the bundled step cannot
// report. One step per file moves that from caught to unrepresentable, because each step's list is
// the one file it names.
//
// THE INCIDENT THAT PAID FOR THIS, carried forward from the bundled step: a PR was blocked by
// sixteen failures in witness families its diff could not reach, the summary counters said fifteen
// identities had moved between dispositions, and it could not be closed because the rows naming
// WHICH fifteen no longer existed anywhere. Two independent recomputations from the trees then
// disagreed with the floor's own counts at both ends -- so the only instrument that could settle
// it was the one the job was deleting. A receipt computed and dropped is worse than one never
// computed, because the summary it backs keeps being quoted as though the detail were available.
//
// long_home_storage_agreement.tsv is in the set deliberately: it is the row recording a file whose
// PATH and declared MODULE disagree about long-home, the first hypothesis any recurrence of that
// incident has to eliminate.
//
// CANCELLATION IS EXCLUDED, a separate fact from the build guard below: a cancelled run's TSVs are
// partial by construction, and a partial receipt read as complete is the fabricated-output
// failure.
fn witness_floor_tsv_upload_step(step_name: String, artifact_name: String, path: String, condition: String) -> Step {
  UsesStep {
    name: Present { value: step_name },
    id: none,
    uses: upload_artifact_action,
    with: Present {
      value: [
        kv(key: "name", value: yaml_string(s: artifact_name)),
        kv(key: "path", value: yaml_string(s: path)),
        kv(key: "if-no-files-found", value: yaml_string(s: "error")),
        kv(key: "retention-days", value: yaml_int(n: 14)),
      ]
    },
    env: none,
    if_condition: Present { value: condition },
    continue_on_error: none,
    timeout_minutes: none
  }
}

// ONE PREPARATION, RENDERED TWICE. Checkout, toolchain and build are byte-identical in both jobs,
// so they are authored ONCE here and each job appends only the step that distinguishes it. Two
// copies would be two definitions of one fact, free to drift the moment either is edited -- the §3
// fork this file already closes for the step/role pair, reintroduced across jobs.
// THE SAME ACTION PROVIDES THE SAME CAPABILITIES WHEREVER IT RUNS. The Install Rust toolchain
// binding below declared only [Cargo, Rustfmt] while gunbc.fleet_workflow_steps ci_setup_rust_step
// declares [Cargo, Rustc, Rustfmt, Rustup] for the identical setup_rust_action -- one action with
// two capability stories, the DESIGN 3 fork. Unnoticed because nothing here consumed Rustup until
// the pin step did, and then the closure refused. The under-declaration is corrected rather than
// the consumer weakened: the action really does install rustup and rustc.
//
// THE TWO LANES BOOTSTRAP DIFFERENTLY AND THAT DIFFERENCE IS THE POINT. The floor lane builds the
// two bins it executes. The BUILD lane builds every declared bin (`--bins`), because host-Rust
// coverage of the whole bin roster is a fact somebody has to establish and this is the lane whose
// subject is "does this repository still build" -- see `repo_self_build_all_bins_command`.
// Duplicating the narrower build in the floor lane would buy nothing: the lanes are
// runtime-independent by construction (no `needs` edge, no artifact handoff), so their bootstraps
// are independent CPU, not a shared prerequisite one could save.
data heal_revalidation_preflight_step_name: String = "Refuse a heal revalidation whose run subject or checkout does not name the expected healed SHA"

// THE PREFLIGHT IS FIRST AFTER CHECKOUT IN EVERY WITNESS-BEARING LANE, and that position is the
// whole claim: a comparison that runs after a witness has already reported cannot unreport it.
// The script is v2.workflow.ci_heal_revalidation_preflight_emit -- orchestration intent, with bash
// as the GitHub Actions foreign-executor realization -- so the refusal arms are modeled control
// flow rather than hand-authored yaml. The heal job does NOT carry it: heal checks out the
// triggering branch head in order to push a repair onto it, so binding that checkout to an
// expected healed sha would refuse the very job that produces one.
fn heal_revalidation_preflight_step() -> Step {
  RunStep {
    name: Present { value: heal_revalidation_preflight_step_name },
    id: none,
    run: ci_heal_revalidation_preflight_script(),
    shell: none,
    env: none,
    working_directory: none,
    if_condition: none,
    continue_on_error: none,
    timeout_minutes: none
  }
}

fn heal_revalidation_preflight_bound_step() -> WitnessFloorBoundStep {
  WitnessFloorBoundStep {
    step: heal_revalidation_preflight_step(),
    role: capability_neutral,
    step_name: heal_revalidation_preflight_step_name,
  }
}

fn prepared_bound_steps(build_script: String) -> List<WitnessFloorBoundStep> {
  [
    WitnessFloorBoundStep {
      step: witness_toolchain_filesystem_start_probe_step(),
      role: capability_neutral,
      step_name: "Record runner filesystem at job start",
    },
    WitnessFloorBoundStep {
      step: witness_floor_checkout_step(),
      role: capability_neutral,
      step_name: "Checkout",
    },
    heal_revalidation_preflight_bound_step(),
    WitnessFloorBoundStep {
      step: toolchain_home_isolation_step(),
      role: capability_neutral,
      step_name: "Isolate toolchain homes",
    },
    WitnessFloorBoundStep {
      step: witness_floor_toolchain_step(),
      role: provides_only(capabilities: [
        CargoCapability, RustcCapability, RustfmtCapability, RustupCapability,
      ]),
      step_name: "Install Rust toolchain",
    },
    WitnessFloorBoundStep {
      step: toolchain_pin_rustup_default_step(),
      role: consumes_only(capabilities: [RustupCapability, CargoCapability]),
      step_name: "Pin rustup default (isolated RUSTUP_HOME has no default toolchain)",
    },
    WitnessFloorBoundStep {
      step: witness_floor_build_step(step_name: witness_floor_build_step_name, build_script: build_script),
      role: consumes_only(capabilities: [CargoCapability]),
      step_name: witness_floor_build_step_name,
    },
  ]
}

// THE FLOOR'S RUN STEP WAS DECLARED `capability_neutral` AND THAT DECLARATION WAS FALSE. It runs
// `claim_executor --required-ci`, whose regen phase spawns rustfmt to normalize the emitted
// mirror, so it consumes RustfmtCapability and now says so.
//
// A step that consumes a capability while declaring none is invisible to the closure check BY
// CONSTRUCTION: the check was honest and had nothing to catch. That is why the failure surfaced as
// a runner lottery instead of a refusal.
//
// Cargo is deliberately NOT listed on the run step: it invokes a prebuilt binary and does not
// shell out to cargo. Listing it would trade one false declaration for another.
fn required_ci_measurement_bound_steps() -> List<WitnessFloorBoundStep> {
  [
    WitnessFloorBoundStep {
      step: required_ci_measurement_finalize_step(), role: capability_neutral,
      step_name: "D0-MEASURE: seal an unreached receipt when the instrument did not return",
    },
    WitnessFloorBoundStep {
      step: witness_floor_tsv_upload_step(
        step_name: "D0-PUBLISH: upload the required CI measurement receipt",
        artifact_name: required_ci_measurement_receipt_artifact_name,
        path: required_ci_measurement_receipt_path,
        condition: required_ci_measurement_publish_condition()
      ),
      role: capability_neutral,
      step_name: "D0-PUBLISH: upload the required CI measurement receipt",
    },
    WitnessFloorBoundStep {
      step: required_ci_measurement_adjudicate_step(), role: capability_neutral,
      step_name: "D0-ADJUDICATE: consume the published measurement receipt",
    },
  ]
}

fn witness_floor_bound_steps() -> List<WitnessFloorBoundStep> {
  append(
    prepared_bound_steps(build_script: witness_floor_build_script()),
    items: concat([
      WitnessFloorBoundStep {
        step: witness_toolchain_homes_isolated_guard_step(),
        role: capability_neutral,
        step_name: "Require isolated toolchain homes",
      },
      WitnessFloorBoundStep {
        step: witness_floor_run_step(),
        role: consumes_only(capabilities: [RustfmtCapability]),
        step_name: "All witnesses (one prepared subject, one fold)",
      },
    ], concat(required_ci_measurement_bound_steps(), [
      WitnessFloorBoundStep {
        step: witness_floor_tsv_upload_step(
          step_name: "Upload the floor's admission roster",
          artifact_name: required_floor_disposition_artifact_name,
          path: required_floor_disposition_path,
          condition: witness_floor_precondition()
        ),
        role: capability_neutral,
        step_name: "Upload the floor's admission roster",
      },
      WitnessFloorBoundStep {
        step: witness_floor_tsv_upload_step(
          step_name: "Upload the expected-red roster join",
          artifact_name: expected_red_roster_join_artifact_name,
          path: expected_red_roster_join_path,
          condition: witness_floor_precondition()
        ),
        role: capability_neutral,
        step_name: "Upload the expected-red roster join",
      },
      WitnessFloorBoundStep {
        step: witness_floor_tsv_upload_step(
          step_name: "Upload the long-home storage agreement",
          artifact_name: long_home_storage_agreement_artifact_name,
          path: long_home_storage_agreement_path,
          condition: witness_floor_precondition()
        ),
        role: capability_neutral,
        step_name: "Upload the long-home storage agreement",
      },
      WitnessFloorBoundStep {
        step: witness_floor_tsv_upload_step(
          step_name: "Upload the floor's per-claim cost receipt",
          artifact_name: required_floor_claim_cost_artifact_name,
          path: required_floor_claim_cost_path,
          condition: witness_floor_precondition()
        ),
        role: capability_neutral,
        step_name: "Upload the floor's per-claim cost receipt",
      },
      WitnessFloorBoundStep {
        step: witness_floor_tsv_upload_step(
          step_name: "Upload the floor's cross-claim demand census",
          artifact_name: required_floor_cross_claim_demand_artifact_name,
          path: required_floor_cross_claim_demand_path,
          condition: witness_floor_precondition()
        ),
        role: capability_neutral,
        step_name: "Upload the floor's cross-claim demand census",
      },
      WitnessFloorBoundStep {
        step: witness_toolchain_filesystem_end_probe_step(),
        role: capability_neutral,
        step_name: "Record runner filesystem at job end",
      },
    ])),
  )
}

// EVERY BINARY TARGET COMPILES, AND IT IS A SECOND STANDING RATHER THAN A WIDER BUILD STEP. Two
// populations were being answered by one roster and only one was ever authored:
//   RuntimeArtifactPopulation -- the executables this job RUNS: claim_executor and gunbc.
//   BinaryCompilePopulation   -- every bin target v1-compiler DECLARES.
// witness_floor_required_bins is the first and is correct as the first; the rule above it
// ("naming a binary that no step runs buys nothing and costs a compile") is about ARTIFACT
// PRODUCTION and stays intact. Nothing answered the second, so a bin outside the runtime pair
// could stop compiling and no required phase would notice.
//
// MEASURED SPECIMEN THAT PRICED THIS STEP: #8952 added `authored_import_names` to TypeEnv and did
// not update `infer_semantics_witness`, whose six synthetic TypeEnv constructions then failed
// E0063. Main stood red at `cargo check -p v1-compiler` and nothing could see it -- the Rust suite
// left CI 2026-07-11, clippy 2026-07-08, the compile-clean gate went with the floor cut, and this
// job builds two of sixteen declared targets. A .dag caller would have refused loudly at resolve;
// a Rust mirror has no such wall once nothing invokes rustc on it, so the exposure is SILENT
// STALENESS rather than a refused closure.
//
// NO NEW ARGV WORD, DELIBERATELY. gunbc.repo_self_build is the single authority for how this
// repository builds itself and its note instructs reviewers to refuse a new argv word the modeled
// surface already owns. An empty bin set already renders the no-selector package build, which
// repo_self_build_empty_bins_note records as cargo's own meaning for "build every target in the
// package" -- so the population is DERIVED from the manifest and a new [[bin]] joins this standing
// with no edit here.
//
// BUILD RATHER THAN CHECK, DECIDED BY MEASUREMENT. From a cold target dir with the two-bin build
// already paid, the marginal cost of building every target was 12s against 52s for `cargo check
// --release --bins`, and the build additionally LINKS. Cheaper and strictly stronger, so there is
// no tradeoff. Re-derive with a fresh CARGO_TARGET_DIR, the two-bin build, then this command.
//
// PLACEMENT IS THE LOAD-BEARING PART, AND IT MOVED FROM THE FLOOR LANE TO THE BUILD LANE. The
// original argument was about ORDER WITHIN THE FLOOR JOB and still holds there: ahead of the fold
// this step would be a preparation mask -- an auxiliary target failing to compile would stop the
// job before the floor ran and the planned/executed/terminal ledger, every semantic measurement
// that job exists to publish, would vanish behind an unrelated compile error. That argument never
// asked WHICH LANE, and the answer it silently assumed was wrong twice over.
//
// FIRST, THE FACT WAS ALREADY ESTABLISHED IN THE OTHER LANE. `build_lane_build_script` is
// `repo_self_build_all_bins_command()` -- `--bins`, every declared bin target -- so the build
// lane's PRELUDE already compiles and links the whole binary population before that lane runs a
// single phase. The floor lane's copy was a second authority for one fact (DESIGN section 3), and
// the two never disagreed because they could not: same package, same profile, same targets.
//
// SECOND, THE DUPLICATE WAS THE ONE ON THE CRITICAL PATH. The floor lane is the longer of the two
// (64m against the build lane's 41m on green main run 33093831916), so this step's 3m13s was pure
// addition to the required check's wall clock. Moving it to the shorter lane removes it from that
// clock. THAT IS THE WHOLE CLAIM, and the only one execution supports.
//
// WHAT THIS STEP DOES NOT DO IS GET CHEAPER, AND AN EARLIER REVISION OF THIS COMMENT SAID IT
// WOULD. It predicted "the 12s marginal case the BUILD-RATHER-THAN-CHECK note above measured",
// reasoning that `build_lane_build_script` is `repo_self_build_all_bins_command()` so every target
// this step names is already built when it runs. MEASURED ON RUN 33107459033, THE FIRST RUN AFTER
// THE MOVE: 3m13s, indistinguishable from its cost in the floor lane. Falsified by execution and
// corrected here rather than left standing: a transcribed figure nothing re-derives is exactly the
// rot DESIGN's name-the-instrument rule exists to stop, and this one was wrong the day it was
// written.
//
// WHY THE PREDICTION FAILED, as a mechanism the lane's own log supports, not a certainty: the
// reasoning looked at the PRELUDE and ignored the 39 minutes between it and this step. Run
// 33107459033's build lane ran `emit-compile` from 19:40:24 to 19:58:54 -- eight emitted closures
// assembled and built through cargo -- and `partition-crates compile` over 7 packages immediately
// after, all sharing this job's target directory. By the time this step runs, the prelude's
// warmth is gone. The 12s figure was measured with the two-bin build paid and NOTHING ELSE
// INTERVENING, which is not this step's position in either lane. CONFIRMED: the duration. The
// eviction is the mechanism that fits the log and is not independently established; do not cite
// it as measured.
//
// THE CONSEQUENCE FOR PLACEMENT IS SMALLER THAN IT LOOKS AND STILL FAVOURS THIS LANE. The move
// buys wall clock on the required check, not compute: the same 3m13s is spent, on a lane with
// roughly 15 minutes of headroom under the floor rather than 23. If the build lane ever overtakes
// the floor lane, this step becomes critical path again and the right response is to re-derive
// both lane durations, not to move it back on the strength of this comment.
//
// WHY MOVED AND NOT DELETED. The two populations above are still two populations, and the build
// lane's prelude answers the second only INCIDENTALLY -- it builds every bin because that is a
// cheap way to produce claim_executor and gunbc, not because anything declares the binary
// population as a standing. Deleting this step would leave that standing resting on a prelude's
// convenience, so a later narrowing of the prelude to the two bins it needs would remove the
// coverage with nothing red to say so. The step survives as the named standing with its own step
// id, verdict and execution provenance; only which job pays for it changed.
//
// THE ANTI-MASK PROPERTY SURVIVES BY CONSTRUCTION RATHER THAN BY ORDERING. In the floor lane it
// was preserved by putting this step last; in the build lane there is no floor ledger to mask, and
// the lanes carry no `needs` edge on each other, so a red here cannot stop the floor from
// publishing its receipts. It keeps `!cancelled()` so a red build lane still reaches it.
//
// WHAT IT ESTABLISHES: every declared v1-compiler binary target compiles and links under the
// release profile. WHAT IT DOES NOT: that any of them RUNS correctly, and nothing about test
// targets, which this command does not build.
// THE BUILD LANE'S BOUND STEPS. Preparation is `prepared_bound_steps` -- the SAME rows the
// witnesses job uses -- and this job appends the two that distinguish it: the environment recorder
// (regen spawns rustfmt, so the probe is wanted exactly where that spawn happens) and the lane's
// run step. The binary-population standing that used to close this lane is gone: its prelude
// already builds every bin (`build_lane_build_script`), so the standing was a second cargo
// invocation asking a question the first had answered. No TSV uploads: the three receipt files
// are written by the FLOOR, so a build-lane job naming them would demand files this lane never
// produces and `if-no-files-found: error` would redden it for a receipt never its to write.
fn build_lane_bound_steps() -> List<WitnessFloorBoundStep> {
  append(
    prepared_bound_steps(build_script: build_lane_build_script()),
    items: [
      WitnessFloorBoundStep {
        step: witness_toolchain_homes_isolated_guard_step(),
        role: capability_neutral,
        step_name: "Require isolated toolchain homes",
      },
      WitnessFloorBoundStep {
        step: build_lane_run_step(),
        role: consumes_only(capabilities: [RustfmtCapability]),
        step_name: "Required CI: build lane (phases named by the run, not by this label)",
      },
      WitnessFloorBoundStep {
        step: rust_clippy_all_targets_step(),
        role: consumes_only(capabilities: [CargoCapability]),
        step_name: "clippy, all targets",
      },
      WitnessFloorBoundStep {
        step: witness_toolchain_filesystem_end_probe_step(),
        role: capability_neutral,
        step_name: "Record runner filesystem at job end",
      },
    ],
  )
}

fn build_lane_steps() -> List<Step> {
  list_map(xs: build_lane_bound_steps(), f: fn(b) { b.step })
}

fn build_lane_step_annotations() -> List<CapabilityAnnotatedStep> {
  list_map(
    xs: build_lane_bound_steps(),
    f: fn(b) { CapabilityAnnotatedStep { step_name: b.step_name, role: b.role } },
  )
}

fn build_lane_capability_closure_holds() -> Bool {
  capability_closure_is_closed(
    v: workflow_job_capability_closure(steps: build_lane_step_annotations()),
  )
}

fn witness_floor_steps() -> List<Step> {
  list_map(xs: witness_floor_bound_steps(), f: fn(b) { b.step })
}

fn witness_floor_step_annotations() -> List<CapabilityAnnotatedStep> {
  list_map(
    xs: witness_floor_bound_steps(),
    f: fn(b) { CapabilityAnnotatedStep { step_name: b.step_name, role: b.role } },
  )
}

fn witness_floor_capability_closure_holds() -> Bool {
  capability_closure_is_closed(
    v: workflow_job_capability_closure(steps: witness_floor_step_annotations()),
  )
}

// THE BEHAVIORAL RECEIPT IS NOT IN THIS JOB AT ALL (operator ruling, 2026-08-21).
//
// It was two steps until #8647 folded it into `--required-ci`, and this paragraph recorded that
// relocation. The ruling then cut it from the required mode entirely, with merge-admission
// capture and stamping and the regen fixed point, so neither a step here nor a phase inside the
// invocation exists.
//
// WHAT THAT MEANS FOR COVERAGE, kept because the fact outlives the mechanism: no required run now
// compares a changed authority against its committed mirror. The comparison survives as
// `--behavioral-receipt-plan` / `--behavioral-receipt-selftest` / `--behavioral-receipt-census`,
// which have no automated consumer, so it is run deliberately or not at all. A declared scope
// narrowing, not a climb; naming it here keeps its absence from reading as coverage.

fn witness_floor_lane_needs() -> List<String> { [] }

fn witness_floor_job() -> Job {
  Job {
    id: floor_lane_job_id,
    name: none,
    runner: gunbc_ci_selected_runner_spec(),
    steps: witness_floor_steps(),
    needs: witness_floor_lane_needs(),
    env: none,
    outputs: none,
    if_condition: none,
    timeout_minutes: minute_count(witness_floor_lane_timeout),
    continue_on_error: none,
    concurrency: none,
    permissions: none
  }
}

// THE LANE'S EXECUTION ENVELOPE, NAMED BECAUSE A SECOND CONSUMER NOW READS IT. The value did not
// change; it stopped being a literal. gunbc.repo_ruleset derives the merge queue's
// check_response_timeout from this row, because a queue whose response timeout is BELOW the
// repository's own execution envelope manufactures queue failures out of runs that were going to
// pass -- it dequeues green work for being slow. Two numbers that must not drift apart are one
// row, read twice (DESIGN section 3).
//
// IT IS A Minute AND NOT AN Int, because the fact is a duration and std.measure already owns that
// carrier. The Actions job field is an Int, so the projection unwraps it at the boundary -- which
// is the right place for the unit to disappear: the YAML key is named `timeout-minutes` and
// carries the unit in its spelling, while the value this row hands to a second consumer carries it
// in its type.
data witness_floor_lane_timeout: Minute = minute(count: 180)

// MERGE_GROUP IS ENROLLED SO THE REQUIRED CHECK CAN RUN ON THE COMPOSED COMMIT, NOT ONLY ON EACH
// BRANCH HEAD. Without this trigger a merge queue would create its merge-group commit and this
// workflow would never schedule for it, leaving the required check pending forever -- the
// prerequisite must exist BEFORE the queue is enabled, not alongside it.
//
// WHY IT IS NEEDED AT ALL, measured. Three pairs of PRs have each been independently green and
// jointly broken main: gunbc#9049 with #9114, #9057 with #9062, and #8919 with #8992. None
// involved a textual conflict: the first change altered a semantic API and the second was checked
// against a base where the old API still existed, so both greens were true of trees that never
// existed together. The #9057/#9062 pair took the whole fleet red for about an hour on 2026-08-24
// and produced four uncoordinated repair PRs plus a fifth branch, two of which proposed deleting
// live code.
//
// THIS ROW IS THE IN-REPOSITORY HALF AND IT IS INERT ON ITS OWN. With no merge queue configured
// the merge_group event never fires, so CI runs unchanged today; it is a no-op until a repository
// setting is changed -- deliberately, so the setting can be flipped without a window in which the
// required check cannot schedule. The other half is a repository setting, and as of
// gunbc.repo_ruleset a DECLARED and OBSERVED one: that module carries
// strict_required_status_checks_policy false as a stated position and refuses on any divergence
// it can read. It still cannot flip a setting nobody has declared -- there is no merge-queue rule
// in desired, because enabling the queue is an operator decision and modelling it as desired
// would apply a policy nobody chose. And no wall inside this repository can constrain what GitHub
// admits to main in any case. Recorded here so the two halves are legible as one change.
//
// THE INVARIANT BOTH HALVES SERVE: the commit admitted to main must be the exact composed commit
// that passed the required checks. A merge queue establishes it by construction; requiring
// branches to be up to date approximates it at the cost of re-running the ~30-minute floor for
// every PR behind a merge.
// A PROVISIONAL BOUND, DERIVED FROM MEASUREMENTS THAT DO NOT YET INCLUDE THIS JOB.
//
// The two phases this lane owns were measured on a session worktree, not a CI runner: regen ~9
// minutes, and the v2 emission compile of `src/v2/compiler/00_compile.dag` ~7 minutes -- ~16
// minutes serial in one job, against the witnesses lane's ~30-40. 90 is above every measured
// sequence with room for a slower runner, and low enough that a hung job's log arrives the same
// working hour -- the witnesses job's 180 reasoning applied to a smaller subject, not copied.
//
// It is NOT derived from a completed CI run of this job, because there has not been one. Calling
// it derived would be the fabricated plausible output DESIGN §5 forbids.
//
// RESTORATION TRIGGER: the first CI run that completes this lane. A real wall time replaces the
// constant and this note dissolves with it.
//
// NO `needs` EDGE, AND THAT IS THE ENTIRE CHANGE. Two jobs naming each other in `needs` are the
// serial run with extra checkouts; two that do not are the parallel one. The lanes share no data
// -- regen's receipt is consumed by nothing outside its own process, and the floor's three TSVs
// are written and uploaded inside the witnesses job -- so there is nothing an ordering edge could
// protect.
// THE ALL-TARGETS LINT PASS, NOW A STEP ON THE BUILD LANE RATHER THAN A JOB OF ITS OWN.
//
// It arrived inside a `rust-unit-tests` job that also ran the unit tests. That job is
// gone under the 2026-09-04 operator ruling on runner capacity: seven jobs against a fleet that
// could not serve them, each paying its own checkout, toolchain install and release build for one
// tree. THE UNIT TESTS WENT WITH IT and are a declared drop -- `gunbc.rung_drop`
// `rust_unit_tests_off_the_merge_path` carries the previous rung, the bounded population and the
// restoration trigger, and is the row to read before concluding those tests are covered.
//
// THE LINT DID NOT GO WITH IT, and that asymmetry is the whole of this comment. Deleting it would
// have been the below-floor regression DESIGN section 4b forbids: `repo_self_clippy_command` is
// the only command on any CI path that compiles the INTEGRATION-TEST AND EXAMPLE targets, which is
// how twelve of them sat red on main (2026-08-30) behind a green required run. A reader tempted to
// finish the job's removal by taking this step too would be re-opening a measured hole, not
// tidying a leftover.
//
// WHY THE BUILD LANE AND NOT THE FLOOR. The floor lane is the longer of the two, so it owns the
// required check's wall clock; a step added there is added to that clock, and one added to the
// build lane is absorbed by its headroom. That is the same argument, and the same instrument, as
// the binary-population standing above -- re-derive both lane durations from the runs before
// moving it, rather than trusting this sentence to still be true.
data rust_clippy_all_targets_shell_emit_dissolution_trigger: DissolutionCondition = unbound_dissolution(description: "dissolve-on: rust_clippy_all_targets_step -- hand-shell `run:` carrier for the one cargo clippy invocation gunbc.repo_self_build repo_self_clippy_command names; the argv is modeled, the step body is a foreign-executor shell line. DISSOLVES WHEN the orchestration-to-shell bash emission (v2.workflow.ci_workflow_run_emit ci_workflow_run_emit_pipeline) renders a modeled cargo Pipeline step for this command, the same capability ci_pin_rustup_default_script waits on -- at which point this data row and the concat below delete together.")

fn rust_clippy_all_targets_step() -> Step {
  RunStep {
    name: Present { value: "clippy, all targets" },
    id: Present { value: "rust_clippy_all_targets" },
    run: concat(
      concat("# ", concat(dissolution_description(condition: rust_clippy_all_targets_shell_emit_dissolution_trigger), "\n")),
      concat(repo_self_clippy_command(), "\n")
    ),
    shell: none,
    env: none,
    working_directory: none,
    if_condition: none,
    continue_on_error: none,
    timeout_minutes: none
  }
}

// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// THE HEAL JOB, RESTORED AS AN EMISSION OF THIS AUTHORITY (gunbc.rung_drop floor_cut_heal).
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// WHAT WAS ACTUALLY MISSING, which is smaller than the row reads and worth stating exactly. The
// 2026-08-15 floor cut deleted `gunbc.ci_workflow` and `ci.yml` with it, and the heal JOB went
// with them. Everything the job DOES survived that cut unconsumed: gunbc.heal_push_plan decides
// per artifact whether the Actions job credential may advance its path, gunbc.ci_spec renders the
// git config / drift census / staging / commit / push script from that decision, and
// gunbc.ci_heal_credential models the checkout step whose persisted credential is the push
// authority. What did not survive was a WORKFLOW that emits a job binding them, so the whole
// model was a check with no consumer -- specification-without-execution, DESIGN §5. This job is
// that consumer, and it is emitted here because this file is now the repository's only CI
// workflow authority.
//
// THIS JOB IS SMALLER THAN THE ONE IN HISTORY AND THAT IS A CLIMB, NOT A CAPABILITY LOSS -- read
// this paragraph before concluding otherwise. `git show 611fd027708^:.github/workflows/ci.yml`
// carries a 223-line heal_generated_artifacts, and FOUR of its arms are absent here:
//
//   * the binary/source revision skew guard (`git rev-list HEAD..$BUILT_FROM -- src/v1`)
//   * its automatic merge remedy (`git merge --no-edit $BUILT_FROM`)
//   * the conflict resolution that took the ours side of a generated artifact
//   * the ~200-path hand-maintained `:(exclude)` roster that classified a conflicting path as
//     authored or generated
//
// All four existed to manage ONE defect, and none of them is a capability in its own right.
// Incident 2026-07-29: the release binary came from a job that checked out the MERGE ref while
// the heal checked out the BRANCH HEAD, so the two could name different revisions of `src/v1`;
// a seed commit adding a Rust->.dag call then made the regen die mid-run on every branch that
// predated it (#7392, #7394, #7395, none at fault). The guard detected that state, the merge
// repaired it, the --ours arm resolved what the merge could not, and the roster decided which
// paths the --ours arm was allowed to touch.
//
// THIS JOB BUILDS ITS OWN `gunbc` FROM THE SAME BRANCH-HEAD CHECKOUT IT THEN REGENERATES, in one
// job, so the binary and the tree are one revision BY CONSTRUCTION. There is no second revision
// to skew against, hence nothing to detect, nothing to merge, no conflict to resolve, and no
// authored-versus-generated classification to make. The class moves from `guarded by a check`
// (DESIGN §4b rung 2) to `has no constructor` (rung 4); the arms are dissolved by the climb, per
// §4b(4), rather than deleted from a job that still needs them.
//
// TWO OF THEM WOULD ALSO BE DEFECTS TO RESTORE, independently of the above, and they are named
// so a future reader does not helpfully rebuild them. Taking the ours side of a diverged
// generated artifact is exactly what the generated-artifact merge driver now REFUSES to do
// (DESIGN, Building & checks: it dropped the other side's authority-derived bytes with no
// conflict, twice on #7836) -- a silent widen, which §5 forbids. And the `:(exclude)` roster was
// a second authority for a fact `gunbc.generated_artifact` already owns, which rots silently
// (§3). The auto-push-versus-author-commit split this job DOES make is derived instead: it folds
// that registry through gunbc.heal_push_plan, and no path is retyped anywhere.
//
// WHY THE BRANCH HEAD AND NOT THE MERGE REF: the job pushes the regeneration back, and a merge
// ref is not a branch anyone can push. `ci_heal_branch_head_checkout_step` is the modeled step
// carrying that, including the persisted-credentials binding gunbc.heal_push_plan resolves the
// push authority from -- so editing the checkout's inputs moves the plan's verdict rather than
// silently breaking the push.
//
// IT MUST NOT BE ADDED TO THE `witnesses` AGGREGATE'S `needs` LIST, and the reason is a role
// split rather than a preference (manager ruling, 2026-09-02). HEAL REMEDIATES; the build lane's
// `generated-artifact` phase ADJUDICATES. Drift is caught by that phase whether or not this job
// ran, so leaving heal out of the required needs does not weaken the gate -- and putting it in
// would turn a remediator into one, at which point its own nonzero SupersededByHealedHead arm
// would block the very branch it had just repaired. It also holds contents:write, and a merge
// gate holding a write credential is a different safety question from the one this row answers.
// So it carries no lane and no `needs` edge: advisory to the merge, load-bearing to the branch.
data heal_generated_artifacts_timeout_minutes: Int = 90

data heal_generated_artifacts_required_bins: List<String> = ["gunbc"]

fn heal_generated_artifacts_build_script() -> String {
  repo_self_build_command(bins: heal_generated_artifacts_required_bins)
}

fn heal_regen_step() -> Step {
  RunStep {
    name: Present { value: "Regenerate every committed generated artifact" },
    id: none,
    run: gunbc_ci_heal_regen_invoke(),
    shell: none,
    env: none,
    working_directory: none,
    if_condition: none,
    continue_on_error: none,
    timeout_minutes: none
  }
}

// THE ONLY MOMENT THE REPAIR IS OBSERVABLE IS BEFORE THE ACTUATOR RUNS. This step reads the
// committed projections while they are still the drifted ones and writes the trailer lines the
// commit step folds into heal's message. Ordering is the whole content of this step: placed after
// the regeneration it would observe a tree that agrees with its authority by construction and
// declare nothing, every time, while still looking like a live mechanism.
fn heal_repair_declaration_step() -> Step {
  RunStep {
    name: Present { value: "Declare which ledger rows this heal will repair" },
    id: none,
    run: gunbc_ci_heal_repair_declaration_invoke(),
    shell: none,
    env: none,
    working_directory: none,
    if_condition: none,
    continue_on_error: none,
    timeout_minutes: none
  }
}

// The generated pre-commit hook re-asserts core.hooksPath and the generated-artifact merge driver
// binding. Converging it here is what makes the commit below run under the same hook configuration
// a developer's clone has, rather than under whatever a fresh runner checkout defaults to.
fn heal_repo_local_git_config_step() -> Step {
  RunStep {
    name: Present { value: "Converge repository-local git config" },
    id: none,
    run: gunbc_ci_heal_repo_local_git_config_invoke(),
    shell: none,
    env: none,
    working_directory: none,
    if_condition: none,
    continue_on_error: none,
    timeout_minutes: none
  }
}

data heal_commit_push_step_id: String = "heal_commit_push"

// GITHUB_TOKEN IS PROJECTED HERE BECAUSE THE DISPATCH READS IT AS A PROCESS VARIABLE, and the
// grant is added WITH its executing consumer rather than in advance of one. GitHub Actions injects
// `github.token` into `secrets.GITHUB_TOKEN` and into the checkout action's credential, but not
// into the step environment; extdeps.github.workflows github.Workflows resolves its bearer solely
// from auth_source EnvVar GITHUB_TOKEN, so without this binding CreateDispatch would refuse 401.
// This is the only step in the workflow that carries it, and it carries it because the heal
// dispatch composed into its script is the only caller.
fn heal_commit_push_step() -> Step {
  RunStep {
    name: Present { value: "Commit and push the regeneration" },
    id: Present { value: heal_commit_push_step_id },
    run: gunbc_ci_heal_commit_push_script(),
    shell: none,
    env: Present {
      value: [kv(key: "GITHUB_TOKEN", value: yaml_string(s: "${{ github.token }}"))]
    },
    working_directory: none,
    if_condition: none,
    continue_on_error: none,
    timeout_minutes: none
  }
}

// THE REFUSED HALF LEAVES WITH ITS BYTES, WHICH IS THE DIFFERENCE BETWEEN A REFUSAL AND A DEAD
// END. A path gunbc.heal_push_plan classifies AuthorCommitRequired -- today the workflow
// projections, because the Actions job credential carries no `workflows` scope -- cannot be pushed
// by this job at all. Printing "regenerate it yourself" and discarding the regenerated bytes would
// make the author redo work the runner already did; uploading them means the remedy is a download
// and a commit. The step that produced them exits nonzero either way, so this is not an escape
// hatch: the line is stopped, and the repair is handed over rather than withheld.
// Workflow projections live under .github, so the uploaded bundle is empty without
// `include-hidden-files`. The live H0 heal for gunbc#10302 proved the failure: it classified
// witnesses.yml as AuthorCommitRequired, copied it below the bundle root, then upload-artifact's
// default hidden-file exclusion reported "No files were found". A refused repair must leave with
// its bytes; include the directory rather than merely printing the path.
fn heal_author_commit_bundle_step() -> Step {
  UsesStep {
    name: Present { value: "Upload the author-commit-required regeneration" },
    id: none,
    uses: upload_artifact_action,
    with: Present {
      value: [
        kv(key: "name", value: yaml_string(s: ci_heal_author_commit_artifact_name)),
        kv(key: "path", value: yaml_string(s: ci_heal_author_commit_artifact_dir)),
        kv(key: "if-no-files-found", value: yaml_string(s: "error")),
        kv(key: "retention-days", value: yaml_int(n: 14)),
        kv(key: "include-hidden-files", value: yaml_bool(b: true)),
      ]
    },
    env: none,
    if_condition: Present {
      value: concat(
        concat(concat("always() && steps.", heal_commit_push_step_id), concat(".outputs.", ci_heal_author_commit_required_output)),
        " == '1'"
      )
    },
    continue_on_error: none,
    timeout_minutes: none
  }
}

// THE REGEN STEP IS capability_neutral, AND THE FLOOR'S RUN STEP IS NOT THE PRECEDENT IT LOOKS
// LIKE. That step declares RustfmtCapability because `claim_executor --required-ci` carries a
// REGEN PHASE that shells to rustfmt to normalize the emitted stage0 mirror. This job's regen step
// runs a different producer: `main_wet` folds `committed_generated_artifacts()` through
// `artifact_generate` and writes the bytes, and nothing on that path spawns a formatter -- the
// emitters that could care author `#[rustfmt::skip]` into their own output instead. Declaring
// rustfmt here by analogy would be the false declaration this file already caught once on the
// floor's run step, in the opposite direction: claiming a consumer that does not exist rather than
// omitting one that does. The toolchain provider is still consumed, by the build step above it.
fn heal_generated_artifacts_bound_steps() -> List<WitnessFloorBoundStep> {
  [
    WitnessFloorBoundStep {
      step: ci_heal_branch_head_checkout_step(),
      role: capability_neutral,
      step_name: "Checkout triggering branch head (heal pushes the regeneration back to this branch)",
    },
    WitnessFloorBoundStep {
      step: toolchain_home_isolation_step(),
      role: capability_neutral,
      step_name: "Isolate toolchain homes",
    },
    WitnessFloorBoundStep {
      step: witness_floor_toolchain_step(),
      role: provides_only(capabilities: [
        CargoCapability, RustcCapability, RustfmtCapability, RustupCapability,
      ]),
      step_name: "Install Rust toolchain",
    },
    WitnessFloorBoundStep {
      step: toolchain_pin_rustup_default_step(),
      role: consumes_only(capabilities: [RustupCapability, CargoCapability]),
      step_name: "Pin rustup default (isolated RUSTUP_HOME has no default toolchain)",
    },
    WitnessFloorBoundStep {
      step: witness_floor_build_step(step_name: heal_build_step_name, build_script: heal_generated_artifacts_build_script()),
      role: consumes_only(capabilities: [CargoCapability]),
      step_name: heal_build_step_name,
    },
    WitnessFloorBoundStep {
      step: heal_repair_declaration_step(),
      role: capability_neutral,
      step_name: "Declare which ledger rows this heal will repair",
    },
    WitnessFloorBoundStep {
      step: heal_regen_step(),
      role: capability_neutral,
      step_name: "Regenerate every committed generated artifact",
    },
    WitnessFloorBoundStep {
      step: heal_repo_local_git_config_step(),
      role: capability_neutral,
      step_name: "Converge repository-local git config",
    },
    WitnessFloorBoundStep {
      step: heal_commit_push_step(),
      role: capability_neutral,
      step_name: "Commit and push the regeneration",
    },
    WitnessFloorBoundStep {
      step: heal_author_commit_bundle_step(),
      role: capability_neutral,
      step_name: "Upload the author-commit-required regeneration",
    },
  ]
}

fn heal_generated_artifacts_capability_closure_holds() -> Bool {
  capability_closure_is_closed(
    v: workflow_job_capability_closure(
      steps: list_map(
        xs: heal_generated_artifacts_bound_steps(),
        f: fn(b) { CapabilityAnnotatedStep { step_name: b.step_name, role: b.role } },
      )
    ),
  )
}

fn heal_generated_artifacts_job() -> Job {
  Job {
    id: heal_generated_artifacts_job_id,
    name: none,
    runner: gunbc_ci_selected_runner_spec(),
    steps: list_map(xs: heal_generated_artifacts_bound_steps(), f: fn(b) { b.step }),
    needs: [],
    env: none,
    outputs: none,
    if_condition: Present { value: ci_regen_heal_if },
    timeout_minutes: heal_generated_artifacts_timeout_minutes,
    continue_on_error: none,
    concurrency: none,
    permissions: Present { value: ci_heal_job_permissions }
  }
}

fn build_lane_needs() -> List<String> { [] }

fn build_lane_job() -> Job {
  Job {
    id: build_lane_job_id,
    name: none,
    runner: gunbc_ci_selected_runner_spec(),
    steps: build_lane_steps(),
    needs: build_lane_needs(),
    env: none,
    outputs: none,
    if_condition: none,
    timeout_minutes: 90,
    continue_on_error: none,
    concurrency: none,
    permissions: none
  }
}

// THE ONE JOB THAT GATES, AND THE ONLY PLACE IN THIS FILE THAT AUTHORS SHELL TEXT.
//
// Every other step renders a modeled value -- an ArgvCommand through `shell_command_render`, or
// `repo_self_build_command` -- and this one cannot: GitHub has no declarative "this job fails
// unless those jobs succeeded". The nearest declarative form, a job-level `if` on the two results,
// is exactly the fail-open being closed -- a job whose `if` is false is SKIPPED, and a skipped
// required check does not stop a merge. So the refusal must be a step that exits nonzero, which is
// shell.
//
// IT IS BUILT FROM THE JOB IDS RATHER THAN SPELLING THEM. A hand-typed `needs.build.result` would
// be a second spelling of a name this file declares, free to drift when either job is renamed --
// SILENTLY, because an unknown `needs` context renders as the empty string, which is not
// `success`, so the gate would refuse every run and read as a broken lane rather than a broken
// reference.
//
// THE JOB-LEVEL CONDITION IS THE LOAD-BEARING ONE, AND THE FIRST CUT OF THE AGGREGATOR PUT IT ONLY
// ON THE STEP. Found in review of gunbc#9203 (review 55795): the same fail-open this job exists to
// close, committed one level in -- the failure mode DESIGN names as total at the level examined
// and blind one level down.
//
// `needs` carries an IMPLICIT JOB-LEVEL CONDITION: a job declaring `needs` and no `if` is SKIPPED
// when any needed job fails, is skipped, or is cancelled. A skipped job never starts, so never
// reaches its steps, so a step-level `always()` cannot rescue it -- and a SKIPPED required check
// does not stop a merge. The aggregator would have gone skipped-and-mergeable over precisely the
// failed lane it was introduced to catch.
//
// So the job declares a condition, displacing that implicit default the same way a status
// function displaces a step's implicit `success()` (the substitution rule the floor's
// precondition paragraph records, and why a bare `needs.*.result` comparison would not have
// worked here either).
//
// IT IS `always()` AND NOT `!cancelled()`, THE ONE PLACE IN THIS FILE THAT DEPARTS FROM THE HOUSE
// GUARD -- stated explicitly so a reader who knows the convention does not correct it back. Every
// other guard decides whether a STEP should run inside a job already running, where
// `!cancelled()` is right. This condition decides whether the REQUIRED CONTEXT EXISTS AT ALL, and
// under `!cancelled()` a cancelled run leaves the aggregator skipped, so the context reports as
// skipped rather than as a verdict.
//
// THE REASON IT IS `always()` IS NOT THE REASON IT USED TO BE, AND THE CHANGE IS THE WHOLE POINT
// OF `gunbc.floor_attempt_standing`. The argument this paragraph used to carry was a choice
// between two bad readings of cancellation -- `always()` treats every cancelled lane as failed,
// `!cancelled()` treats every cancelled workflow as lawful supersession -- and picked `always()`
// because noise on an abandoned head beats silence on a live one. That trade was real, and forced
// only because ONE CONDITION WAS BEING ASKED TO CARRY TWO QUESTIONS.
//
// It is not forced any more. Head-standing and lane-terminality are two independent axes, and the
// job condition owns NEITHER: only whether this job runs. `always()` is the right answer to THAT
// question for a reason unrelated to cancellation semantics -- a required context that can be
// SKIPPED turns mergeability on an unmeasured third-party behaviour, and under `always()` skipped
// disappears from the context entirely. Construction over validation (DESIGN 5): the ambiguous
// state becomes unrepresentable rather than tested.
//
// WHAT ANSWERS THE CANCELLATION QUESTION IS NOW THE GATE'S OWN BODY, where both axes are visible
// at once. `cancelled` with a measured head no longer the pull request's head is a SUPERSEDED
// ATTEMPT and does not block; `cancelled` with a measured head that IS still the pull request's
// head is `stands-unestablished` -- evidence missing on a live merge candidate -- and blocks, with
// an annotation saying it is an infrastructure event rather than a subject defect. Neither
// `cancelled -> benign` nor `cancelled -> failed` is asserted as a universal reading, which is
// exactly what both previous shapes did.
//
// WHAT IS NOT YET TRUE OF THAT, THE HALF A READER MUST NOT ASSUME: the first arm is not reachable
// in production today, because nothing in this workflow acquires the pull request's CURRENT head.
// The gate therefore renders head-standing as `unobserved` on every pull-request run and takes the
// fail-closed arm. The BLOCKING PREDICATE is unchanged -- block iff either lane is not `success`
// -- while the annotation and the receipt say considerably more. Which runs block is identical;
// what they print is not. What changed today is the SPLIT BETWEEN RED AND UNESTABLISHED, which
// needs no acquisition, and the attempt subject printed on every run, which makes the head join
// computable offline by anyone who can read the API. `gunbc.floor_attempt_standing`
// `floor_attempt_standing_acquisition_trigger` carries what closes the rest and what goes red when
// it does.
//
// THE COST OF `always()` IS UNCHANGED AND STILL PAID: a lawfully superseded run reports this
// context red, because a cancelled lane is not `success`. Measured on run 32883390033, where a
// fleet event ended both lanes and this job published `failure`. That red now says
// `verdict=stands-unestablished`, carries mechanism and attribution, and names the measured head
// it belongs to -- strictly more than before -- but it is still a red on an abandoned head, and
// only the head acquisition removes it.
//
// WHY THAT COST IS ACCEPTED RATHER THAN TRADED AWAY: THE ASYMMETRY OF WHICH HEAD PAYS. Carried
// over from the interim's own authority (gunbc#9302, closed in favour of this construction)
// because it survives the change of mechanism and is the deciding fact. Checks are tracked per
// head SHA, so a superseded run's red lands on a head that BY CONSTRUCTION will never be merged --
// the superseding push created the head that will, with its own run. `always()` is therefore
// NOISY ON ABANDONED HEADS. The alternative moves the cost onto the LIVE one: a lane killed with
// no replacement run coming -- observed twice on 2026-08-25, once with `runner_name` empty and
// zero steps, once with both lanes ended at 18:43 and no push involved -- leaves this context
// SKIPPED on the head that is still the merge candidate.
//
// NOISE IS A COST; FAIL-OPEN IS A DEFECT (operator ruling relayed by smart-ram-730, 2026-08-26,
// deciding between the two shapes). A loud wrong-looking red gets investigated and corrected. A
// silent skip on an unverified live head does not, and it is the one failure mode nobody sees.
//
// AND THE NOISE IS MATERIALLY REDUCED WITHOUT THE ACQUISITION, which makes this a bearable trade
// rather than a pious one. The harm the interim was chartered against was people investigating
// FALSE SUBJECT FAILURES, and a run reporting `stands-unestablished` with a mechanism and an
// attribution sends nobody into a diff. What is left is a red on a head nobody will merge, the
// cheap half of the original complaint.

// RETIRING gunbc#9339's CANCELLED-LANE EXCLUSION, AND SAYING SO RATHER THAN DELETING IT QUIETLY.
//
// That change landed while this one was in review and added
// `&& needs.build.result != 'cancelled' && needs.floor.result != 'cancelled'` here, under a
// declared DESIGN 4b(3) rung drop on a `required_lanes_aggregate_cancellation_rung_drop` row that
// this change deletes with the exclusion it declared. It was the right interim: with only two
// lane-result words it could not separate the modal lawful supersession from an abandoned live
// attempt, so it removed the false alert at the only distinction available and DECLARED the
// resulting fail-open on `CurrentHeadUnestablished` instead of hiding it.
//
// ITS OWN RESTORATION TRIGGER NAMES THIS CONSTRUCTION BY NAME -- "the operator-ranked
// floor-current-head-binding: FloorAttemptSubject carries pull_request, measured_head, run_id;
// FloorHeadStanding is Current, Superseded or Unobservable independently of FloorLaneTerminal".
// So the exclusion and its declaration are removed here as a dissolution on climb (DESIGN 4b(4)):
// production machinery deleted, evidence kept -- the consolidation witnesses that pinned the
// exclusion are re-pointed at `always()` in the same change rather than dropped.
//
// WHAT IS HONESTLY RESTORED AND WHAT IS NOT, because taking the trigger's name is not discharging
// it. RESTORED: the fail-open. A cancelled lane on a live head reaches this gate again and blocks
// as `stands-unestablished`, the half the interim declared it was giving up. NOT DISCHARGED: the
// supersession OBSERVATION. Nothing here acquires the pull request's current head, so a lawfully
// superseded run pays a red exactly as before the interim -- the noise the interim was chartered
// to remove comes back. That is the operator's ruling, not this file's choice: NOISE IS A COST,
// FAIL-OPEN IS A DEFECT. The remaining half of #9339's trigger survives unretired as
// `gunbc.floor_attempt_standing` `floor_attempt_standing_acquisition_trigger`, the same obligation
// under the carrier that can actually hold it.
//
// WHY THE EXCLUSION IS NOT MERELY WEAKER BUT ACTIVELY WRONG ON TODAY'S EVIDENCE, measured on this
// PR's own branch after #9339 landed: cancellation here is a BACKGROUND RATE, not a supersession
// signal. Twelve cancelled jobs across 45 minutes of one afternoon, elapsed 7s to 3062s,
// uncorrelated with duration and with any instant; seven floor jobs ran LONGER than the cancelled
// ones and passed. Two of those cancellations were live heads with no replacement run -- exactly
// the cell #9339 declared it was leaving uncovered -- and under its exclusion both would have left
// the required context skipped on a head still the merge candidate. The exclusion treats
// `cancelled` as evidence of supersession; the measurement says it is evidence of nothing.
fn required_lanes_aggregate_job_condition() -> String {
  "always()"
}
//
// THE STEP-LEVEL `always()` STAYS, guarding a different thing. It is about EARLIER STEPS IN THIS
// JOB: none today, so the implicit `success()` would be true anyway, but a step added ahead of it
// later would silently acquire the power to skip the refusal. Two conditions, two failure modes,
// neither a second spelling of the other.
// THE EXPRESSIONS ARE BUILT FROM THE MODELED ACTIONS EXPRESSION GRAMMAR, NOT SPELLED AS TEXT.
//
// This function nested `concat` over `"${{ needs."` and `".result }}"` -- a second spelling of a
// language `extdeps.github.expressions` already models, the same medium-as-string violation that
// moved the gate's shell into `gunbc.required_lanes_gate`, surviving one layer up in the
// expressions the shell interpolates. `interpolate` over a `ContextAccess` renders the identical
// text through the authority that owns the spelling, so the interpolation delimiters, the path
// separator and the context names have one home.
fn required_lane_result_expression(job_id: String) -> String {
  interpolate(expression: ContextAccess { context: Needs, path: [job_id, "result"] })
}

// THE ATTEMPT SUBJECT'S EXPRESSIONS. `github.event.pull_request.*` renders as the empty string on
// push, merge_group and workflow_dispatch runs, and that emptiness is what the gate reads to
// decide the head axis is inapplicable rather than unmeasured.
fn required_lanes_pull_request_expression() -> String {
  interpolate(expression: ContextAccess { context: Github, path: ["event", "pull_request", "number"] })
}

fn required_lanes_measured_head_expression() -> String {
  interpolate(
    expression: ContextAccess { context: Github, path: ["event", "pull_request", "head", "sha"] }
  )
}

fn required_lanes_event_name_expression() -> String {
  interpolate(expression: ContextAccess { context: Github, path: ["event_name"] })
}

fn required_lanes_run_id_expression() -> String {
  interpolate(expression: ContextAccess { context: Github, path: ["run_id"] })
}

// THE COMPARISON IS `!= "success"` AND NOT A LIST OF BAD STATES, now a measured fact rather than a
// preference. Run 32883390033 (2026-08-25) had both lanes ended by a fleet event with no push
// involved, and the aggregator read:
//
//     BUILD="cancelled"   FLOOR="abandoned"
//
// `abandoned` is a `needs.*.result` value nobody here knew was reachable. An enumerated guard --
// `== "failure" || == "cancelled"`, the form instinct reaches for -- would have admitted it and
// reported the required context GREEN over two lanes that never ran. The strict inequality admits
// exactly one state and refuses every other, including ones the author has never heard of: the
// difference between a closed vocabulary and a remembered one (DESIGN §5).
//
// The same run is this gate's first executing receipt: the aggregator ran under `always()` with
// both lanes non-success, refused, and published `witnesses` as a FAILURE -- the behaviour the job
// exists for and the pre-repair shape could not produce.
// THE SHELL IS BUILT AS NODES AND SERIALIZED THROUGH THE GRAMMAR, never spelled as text. The first
// cut nested `concat` over string literals and handed the result to a RunStep -- the
// medium-as-string violation DESIGN 2/3 names, raised in review 55836. Node construction lives in
// `gunbc.required_lanes_gate`; this function is the realization edge, following the
// `tools.build_step` -> `v2.workflow.bash_command_fold_serialize` precedent already in tree.
//
// THE REJECTED ARM REFUSES RATHER THAN RENDERING NOTHING, AND IS NOW BUILT FROM THE SAME GRAMMAR
// AS THE GATE. It was a raw shell String here -- an executable program spelled as text, the
// medium-as-string violation this PR otherwise dissolves, surviving in the one arm nobody reads
// (review 55923). The refusal program is now Nodes in `gunbc.required_lanes_gate` beside the gate
// it stands in for, serialized through the same fold.
//
// THE OUTCOME IS THREE-STATE, NOT TWO, and the third state removes the last fabricated value: gate
// serializes -> the gate; gate rejects but the refusal serializes -> the refusal, which stops every
// run loudly; both reject -> ABSENT, and `expected_witness_floor_yml` refuses to emit any yaml at
// all. A `Rejected` fold can therefore never reach a published step.
data required_lanes_gate_build_var: String = "BUILD"
data required_lanes_gate_floor_var: String = "FLOOR"

// THE REQUIRED LANE ROSTER, AND IT IS ONE ROSTER READ TWICE RATHER THAN TWO LISTS THAT AGREE.
//
// The gate's verdict and the step's `env` block are the same fact asked in two directions -- which
// jobs decide the merge -- and they were previously two hand-kept lists whose agreement nothing
// checked. A lane present here but absent from `needs` reads an unset variable; a lane in `needs`
// but absent here is a job that runs and cannot block. Both are silent. Deriving both surfaces
// from this row makes the second state unwritable and leaves only the `needs` edge to keep in
// step, which is the one edge GitHub owns rather than us.
//
// NON-EMPTY BY CONSTRUCTION -- see `RequiredLane` in `gunbc.required_lanes_gate` for why an empty
// roster is a fail-open rather than a degenerate case.
fn required_lanes_roster() -> FreeSemigroup<RequiredLane> {
  FreeSemigroup {
    head: RequiredLane {
      job_id: build_lane_job_id,
      label: "build",
      var_name: required_lanes_gate_build_var
    },
    tail: [
      RequiredLane {
        job_id: floor_lane_job_id,
        label: "floor",
        var_name: required_lanes_gate_floor_var
      }
    ]
  }
}
data required_lanes_gate_pull_request_var: String = "PULL_REQUEST"
data required_lanes_gate_event_name_var: String = "EVENT_NAME"
data required_lanes_gate_measured_head_var: String = "MEASURED_HEAD"
data required_lanes_gate_run_id_var: String = "RUN_ID"
data required_lanes_gate_standing_var: String = "HEAD_STANDING"
data required_lanes_gate_verdict_var: String = "VERDICT"
data required_lanes_gate_mechanism_var: String = "MECHANISM"
data required_lanes_gate_attribution_var: String = "ATTRIBUTION"

fn required_lanes_aggregate_script_outcome() -> String? {
  match bash_fold_serialize_program(
    stmts: required_lanes_gate_stmts(
      lanes: required_lanes_roster(),
      pull_request_var: required_lanes_gate_pull_request_var,
      event_name_var: required_lanes_gate_event_name_var,
      measured_head_var: required_lanes_gate_measured_head_var,
      run_id_var: required_lanes_gate_run_id_var,
      standing_var: required_lanes_gate_standing_var,
      verdict_var: required_lanes_gate_verdict_var,
      mechanism_var: required_lanes_gate_mechanism_var,
      attribution_var: required_lanes_gate_attribution_var
    ),
    set_e: false
  ) {
    Accepted { value: script, diagnostics: _ } => Present { value: script }
    Rejected { diagnostics: _ } => match bash_fold_serialize_program(
      stmts: required_lanes_gate_unrenderable_stmts(),
      set_e: false
    ) {
      Accepted { value: refusal, diagnostics: _ } => Present { value: refusal }
      Rejected { diagnostics: _ } => none
    }
  }
}

fn required_lanes_gate_is_renderable() -> Bool {
  match required_lanes_aggregate_script_outcome() {
    Present { value: _ } => true
    Absent => false
  }
}

// THE EMPTY STRING HERE IS NOT A PROGRAM AND IS NOT EMITTED. It is reachable only when
// `required_lanes_gate_is_renderable` is false, and that conjunct makes
// `expected_witness_floor_yml` return `WitnessFloorGenerationRefused`, so no yaml carrying it can
// exist. Daglang is total, so SOME value must inhabit the arm; what matters is that it is not a
// second spelling of a shell program and that no emission path reaches it.
fn required_lanes_aggregate_script() -> String {
  match required_lanes_aggregate_script_outcome() {
    Present { value: script } => script
    Absent => ""
  }
}

// THE SUBJECT VALUES ARE BOUND AS ENVIRONMENT, WHICH IS THE ONLY PLACE AN EXPRESSION AND A SHELL
// PROGRAM MAY MEET.
//
// A `${{ }}` expression is substituted into the workflow document before any shell exists, so it
// must reach the emitted yaml verbatim. A yaml scalar does that -- `emit_inline_scalar_text`
// renders an expression unquoted and unescaped -- while a bash literal word cannot, because
// quoting it is exactly what the bash serializer is for. `gunbc.required_lanes_gate` therefore
// takes variable NAMES only and reads them as shell variables; the expressions live here, one row
// per variable, joined to the gate by the same declarations the gate is handed.
fn required_lanes_aggregate_step_env() -> List<YamlKeyValue> {
  list_append(
    left: list_map(xs: non_empty_to_list(xs: required_lanes_roster()), f: fn(lane) {
      kv(
        key: lane.var_name,
        value: yaml_string(s: required_lane_result_expression(job_id: lane.job_id))
      )
    }),
    right: [
    kv(key: required_lanes_gate_pull_request_var, value: yaml_string(s: required_lanes_pull_request_expression())),
    kv(key: required_lanes_gate_event_name_var, value: yaml_string(s: required_lanes_event_name_expression())),
    kv(key: required_lanes_gate_measured_head_var, value: yaml_string(s: required_lanes_measured_head_expression())),
    kv(key: required_lanes_gate_run_id_var, value: yaml_string(s: required_lanes_run_id_expression()))
    ]
  )
}

fn required_lanes_aggregate_step() -> Step {
  RunStep {
    name: Present { value: "Every required lane must have succeeded" },
    id: none,
    run: required_lanes_aggregate_script(),
    shell: none,
    env: Present { value: required_lanes_aggregate_step_env() },
    working_directory: none,
    if_condition: Present { value: "always()" },
    continue_on_error: none,
    timeout_minutes: none
  }
}

// It checks out nothing, installs nothing and builds nothing: it reads two results. A five minute
// bound is an order of magnitude above anything that can take, and stops a stuck aggregator from
// holding a runner for the inherited six hours.
//
// TWO RESULTS AND NOT THREE OR FOUR, AND EACH ABSENCE IS A DECLARED DROP RATHER THAN AN OVERSIGHT.
// #9704 made `fabric-evidence` a needs edge here; #10036-era work made `rust-unit-tests` a third
// required lane. Neither job exists now. `fabric-evidence` lost its edge under `gunbc.rung_drop`
// `fabric_evidence_gating` and then lost the job itself under the 2026-09-04 runner-capacity
// ruling recorded in that same row; the unit tests left the merge path under
// `rust_unit_tests_off_the_merge_path`. A reader who wants either lane back should read the row
// first: both name a CAPABILITY as the trigger -- an evidence process that resolves its entry
// without re-paying the whole-tree census, and runner supply that admits a lane at all -- and
// neither is retired by a pull request that merely re-adds the job.
fn required_lanes_aggregate_job() -> Job {
  Job {
    id: witness_floor_workflow_job_id,
    name: none,
    runner: gunbc_ci_selected_runner_spec(),
    steps: [required_lanes_aggregate_step()],
    needs: list_map(xs: non_empty_to_list(xs: required_lanes_roster()), f: fn(lane) { lane.job_id }),
    env: none,
    outputs: none,
    if_condition: Present { value: required_lanes_aggregate_job_condition() },
    timeout_minutes: 5,
    continue_on_error: none,
    concurrency: none,
    permissions: none
  }
}

// THE TRIGGERS AND THE LANE JOBS ARE NAMED SEPARATELY FROM THE WORKFLOW THEY ASSEMBLE, AND THE
// REASON IS COST, NOT TASTE. A record is materialized whole, so a reader that wants one field pays
// for every other -- and one of the other fields is `required_lanes_aggregate_job`, whose `run` is
// the serialized `gunbc.required_lanes_gate` program, the largest bash AST this workflow carries.
// Measured with `claim_batch --functions`, one witness per subject. What transfers is the shape. The assembled workflow is dominated by one job:
// `required_lanes_aggregate_job` is about seven eighths of its cost and the six lane jobs together
// are the remaining eighth. So a witness asking "is the dispatch input declared" was serializing a
// gate script it says nothing about, and three such rows exceeded `v2.workflow.required_floor`
// `required_floor_claim_cpu_safety_limit_ms` on that check.
//
// AN EARLIER REVISION OF THIS PARAGRAPH CLAIMED A FIXED "FLOOR FOR REACHING THIS MODULE AT ALL",
// AND THERE IS NO SUCH FLOOR. It was inferred from a first probe in which all six per-job subjects
// landed in one narrow band, and a band across six readers is evidence of work SHARED BETWEEN them,
// never of a cost beneath them -- only a reader that touches almost nothing can tell those two
// apart. THE 3ms ROW IS THAT READER AND IT IS WHY THE QUESTION IS SETTLED:
// `test.claim.heal_revalidation_binding_witness`
// `the_required_workflow_declares_the_healed_sha_dispatch_input` reads `witness_floor_triggers` and
// nothing else, and costs two orders of magnitude less than the band on the same instrument. A
// future author pricing a witness against this carrier needs that row and not the phantom, because
// the phantom says a cheap reader is impossible and the row is one.
//
// THIS PARAGRAPH ALSO CARRIED A "THE CONTAINER RUNS ABOUT THREE TIMES THE RUNNER" CALIBRATION, AND
// IT WAS FALSE. It was derived by dividing a local figure by the CI number
// `marginal_cpu_ns=496786520` from check 100530537497 -- a fill that was INTERRUPTED AT THE BUDGET.
// AN INTERRUPTED MEASUREMENT IS NOT A SLOW MEASUREMENT; IT IS A NON-MEASUREMENT WEARING A NUMBER,
// and the number it wears is approximately the ceiling that stopped it. So the ratio described the
// ceiling, not the machine. It reads as data because it has units, which is the same shape as
// budget-refused being UNDECIDED rather than a weak green.
//
// THE METHOD THAT REFUTED IT IS THE SAME-ROW COMPARISON, and only that method could have: on
// `test.claim.heal_revalidation_binding_witness`
// `every_ordinary_checkout_lane_preflights_before_its_first_witness`, local 409ms against 392ms on
// runner srv4-04 at check 100560747021 -- a ratio of about one -- while a sibling row measured
// cheaper locally than on the runner, so the runner is not uniformly faster in either direction. A
// cross-row ratio cannot see that. Cite `eval_steps` for anything that must survive a runner
// change: that row is 148297 steps, and steps are host-independent where milliseconds are not.
//
// THAT ROW SITS AT 392ms UNDER A 500ms CEILING AND THE COST IS NOT REDUCIBLE WITHOUT LOSING THE
// CLAIM. Its subject IS the six lanes -- "every witness-bearing lane" quantifies over them, and a
// roster-backed version would be a worse artifact at any price, since a roster stops being a
// denominator the moment someone adds a lane. THE MODELLED ALTERNATIVE IS NOT AVAILABLE, checked
// rather than assumed: `v2.workflow.required_floor` `required_floor_claim_cpu_safety_limit_ms` is a
// single constant and is not keyed by identity, so no row can be given its own ceiling; the
// interpreter's own remedy sentence asks for a lane that declares a dated ceiling AND names the row
// as an executing consumer; and `gunbc.fleet.fleet_converge_plan` records that no such lane exists
// that executes anything, with relocation into a non-executing home being the bare de-enrollment
// the admission ruling forbids. That work is filed at node://adhoc-cfdf3366-bc3 and is this margin's
// TRIGGER: when a lane with a dated ceiling executes enrolled identities and returns a
// candidate-bound terminal verdict, this row belongs in it. Until then the margin is 108ms on the
// runner named above, and it is recorded here so the next author meets a known bound rather than
// discovering a red.
//
// SPLITTING THE ROWS WOULD NOT HAVE FIXED THAT AND WAS TRIED FIRST. Two rows became four, each half
// landing nearer the ceiling, and the fold they both paid was untouched -- an improvement in the
// arithmetic that was a coincidence of size, one emitted line from red again. DESIGN section 2 says
// to minimize the demand graph before materializing its answers; the demand here was for the
// aggregate's serialized text, and no consumer of these two producers asks for it.
//
// NEITHER IS A SECOND AUTHORITY FOR WHAT THE WORKFLOW IS. `witness_floor_workflow` is assembled
// from exactly these, so a lane added here is a lane in the emitted yaml, and a reader folding
// `witness_floor_lane_jobs` covers every witness-bearing lane by construction rather than by a
// hand-listed roster that could fall behind.
fn witness_floor_triggers() -> List<WorkflowTrigger> {
  [
    WorkflowDispatch { inputs: [ci_heal_expected_healed_sha_input] },
    MergeGroup,
    Push { branches: [gunbc_default_branch_name], paths: [] },
    PullRequest {
      branches: [gunbc_default_branch_name],
      types: [Opened, Synchronize, Reopened]
    }
  ]
}

// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// ADDING A JOB TO THIS LIST REQUIRES OPERATOR SIGN-OFF. IT IS NOT AN ORDINARY EDIT.
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// A job here is not a line of configuration, it is a standing claim on a runner that someone pays
// for, on every push and every pull request, forever. This list was SEVEN on 2026-09-04 and the
// fleet could not serve seven: the required check's wall is the max over its lanes, so jobs that
// gated nothing were displacing the ones that do, and the queue -- not any lane's own cost -- was
// what people waited on. The 2026-09-04 operator ruling cut `rust-unit-tests`, `fabric-evidence`
// and `emit-copy-qualification-battery` on that basis; the drop rows carry what each cut cost.
//
// SO THE ROSTER IS CLOSED TO GROWTH BY DEFAULT, and an author proposing a new lane owes the
// operator three things BEFORE the edit, not in review of it: the MEASURED wall of the job on a
// fleet runner (not an estimate, and not a timeout, which says nothing about what the aggregate
// waits for); what its RED DISCRIMINATES -- a lane that reds for host or shared reasons is worse
// added than absent, because it converts a known-uninformative signal into repository-wide noise;
// and why the check cannot be a STEP on a lane that already checks out and builds this tree, which
// is where every one of these jobs' preludes is otherwise paid a second time.
//
// THAT LAST QUESTION IS THE ONE THAT ACTUALLY SHRINKS THIS LIST. A separate job buys isolation and
// a separate verdict, and it costs a whole checkout, toolchain install and release build of the
// same tree. `rust_clippy_all_targets_step` is the worked example: it kept its step id, its
// verdict and its coverage, and stopped costing a job, by moving onto `required-witnesses-build`.
//
// THIS COMMENT IS RATIONALE, NOT A GATE. No `Accepted` program reads it, so it stops nobody --
// it records WHY the roster is small so the next author meets the reasoning rather than
// rediscovering the outage. The construction that would make an over-budget roster unwritable is
// a runner-wall budget refused at emit time, and it is not built; until it is, this is diligence.
//
// THE AGGREGATE IS DELIBERATELY NOT A MEMBER. It checks out nothing and runs no witness: it reads
// the other lanes' results. Every consumer of this list is asking about lanes that carry a subject,
// and the aggregate joined that population only as something to filter back out.
type WitnessFloorLane = WitnessFloorBuildLane | WitnessFloorFloorLane | WitnessFloorHealLane

fn witness_floor_lane_roster() -> FreeSemigroup<WitnessFloorLane> {
  FreeSemigroup {
    head: WitnessFloorBuildLane,
    tail: [WitnessFloorFloorLane, WitnessFloorHealLane]
  }
}

fn witness_floor_lane_job(lane: WitnessFloorLane) -> Job {
  match lane {
    WitnessFloorBuildLane => build_lane_job()
    WitnessFloorFloorLane => witness_floor_job()
    WitnessFloorHealLane => heal_generated_artifacts_job()
  }
}

fn witness_floor_lane_jobs() -> List<Job> {
  list_map(xs: non_empty_to_list(xs: witness_floor_lane_roster()), f: fn(lane) {
    witness_floor_lane_job(lane: lane)
  })
}

// THE IDENTITY PROJECTION IS NARROW ON PURPOSE. A witness deciding which jobs exist must not
// materialize every job's steps (and therefore the compiler-floor command) merely to read ids.
// Both this projection and the materialized jobs derive from `witness_floor_lane_roster`, so an
// added lane cannot enter one without entering the other.
fn witness_floor_lane_job_id(lane: WitnessFloorLane) -> String {
  match lane {
    WitnessFloorBuildLane => build_lane_job_id
    WitnessFloorFloorLane => floor_lane_job_id
    WitnessFloorHealLane => heal_generated_artifacts_job_id
  }
}

fn witness_floor_lane_job_ids() -> List<String> {
  list_map(xs: non_empty_to_list(xs: witness_floor_lane_roster()), f: fn(lane) {
    witness_floor_lane_job_id(lane: lane)
  })
}

data witness_floor_workflow: Workflow = {
  name: witness_floor_workflow_name,
  run_name: none,
  on: witness_floor_triggers(),
  concurrency: Present {
    value: concurrency_of_policy(
      policy: witness_floor_concurrency_policy,
      group: witness_floor_concurrency_group)
  },
  env: Present {
    value: [
      kv(key: "CARGO_TERM_COLOR", value: yaml_string(s: "always")),
      kv(key: "GUNBC_EXPECTED_RED_ROSTER_JOIN", value: yaml_string(s: expected_red_roster_join_path)),
      kv(key: "GUNBC_REQUIRED_FLOOR_DISPOSITION", value: yaml_string(s: required_floor_disposition_path)),
      kv(key: "GUNBC_LONG_HOME_STORAGE_AGREEMENT", value: yaml_string(s: long_home_storage_agreement_path)),
      kv(key: "GUNBC_REQUIRED_FLOOR_CLAIM_COST", value: yaml_string(s: required_floor_claim_cost_path)),
      kv(key: "GUNBC_REQUIRED_FLOOR_CROSS_CLAIM_DEMAND", value: yaml_string(s: required_floor_cross_claim_demand_path)),
      kv(key: (required_ci_contract_epoch_env_key as String), value: yaml_string(s: (required_ci_contract_epoch as String)))
    ]
  },
  permissions: Present {
    value: WorkflowPermissions {
      contents: Present { value: PermRead },
      pull_requests: none,
      issues: none,
      actions: none,
      id_token: none
    }
  },
  jobs: list_append(left: witness_floor_lane_jobs(), right: [required_lanes_aggregate_job()])
}

// THE CLOSURE HAS A PRODUCTION CONSUMER, which is the difference between a check and a claim. A
// capability check only tests call is specification-without-execution: green forever while the
// emitter produces the broken workflow beside it (DESIGN §5).
//
// So emission refuses. A job whose cargo consumer has no provider ordered before it yields the
// refusal marker instead of yaml -- not a warning, not yaml-with-a-comment, because the
// generated-artifact drift gate compares bytes and a refusal marker cannot be mistaken for a
// workflow. This is the deploy.yml failure made unemittable rather than detectable: that workflow
// ran `cargo build` as step two with no toolchain provider anywhere in the file, and nothing in
// this repository could refuse it.

// THE CLOSURE IS CHECKED PER JOB, AND BOTH JOBS ARE CHECKED. A capability is provided by a step
// INSIDE a job -- the toolchain install does not reach across a job boundary -- so a
// whole-workflow closure would be the wrong shape, and checking only the job that existed first
// would leave the new one guarded by nothing while the emission still refused on its behalf.
// That is the inert half DESIGN §6 names: machinery that exists and gates nothing.
data witness_floor_capability_closure_per_job_note: String = "expected_witness_floor_yml conjoins required_lanes_gate_is_renderable and every job capability closure, one conjunct per member of witness_floor_lane_jobs; adding a job without adding its conjunct would emit that job unchecked."

type WitnessFloorGenerationOutcome
  = WitnessFloorGenerated { content: String }
  | WitnessFloorGenerationRefused { reason: String }

// THE REFUSAL NOW CARRIES ITS CAUSE. It did not before: gunbc.generated_artifact_emit named one
// static string for every arm, so a toolchain-home refusal and a capability-closure refusal reached
// the operator as the same sentence. The two have nothing in common but the file they suppress.
data witness_floor_capability_closure_refusal_reason: String = "REFUSED gunbc.witness_floor_workflow: a lane's capability closure does not hold, or the required-lanes gate is not renderable. No workflow yaml was emitted. Fix the step roles in the lane's annotations, or the order of the steps themselves."

// THE FOLD'S FIRST REAL EXECUTION REFUSED, AND THAT IS THE FINDING. Run on GitHub (12m24s whole
// job) and reproduced locally (531s for the fold alone): strict preparation refused with one typed
// located diagnostic and ZERO witnesses executed. Cost shape inside the fold — frontend 38s,
// normalize 2s, reconcile ~8 minutes, analyses 1s; reconcile IS the fold. Diagnostic census after
// repairing this cut's own residue: 282 diagnostics across 75 files (57 test/fixture, 18
// production). CORRECTION 2026-08-15: this sentence first read that ZERO of them referenced
// anything this cut deleted. FALSE, and the method is the error worth keeping: the check was a
// grep of the refusal set for `ci_floor|falsifier|selection|scoped_witness|pr_native|fast_lane` —
// names I expected — so it could only find residue already thought of. Two carriers declared in
// the deleted `affected_set_floor_runner` (`FloorDiffLineTouch`, `DiffPathsProduced`) matched none
// and sat in the census unattributed, with the two witness files that consumed them. The sound
// check asks from the other side: for every unresolved symbol in the refusal set, is it declared
// anywhere in the live tree, and if not, was it declared in a file this branch deleted. That found
// them immediately. A grep for names you predict is not a census but a confirmation of your own
// list — the second time in one day this lane produced an incomplete enumeration while documenting
// the incomplete-enumeration class. Against the old floor's 56.5 minutes this is 8m51s, but the
// comparison is NOT like-for-like and must not be quoted as one: a refusing run that executed
// nothing is cheaper than one that executed 9,000 witnesses for reasons unrelated to the fold's
// design.

// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// FIRST-EXECUTION FINDINGS (2026-08-15). Three rulings, recorded here beside the emission they
// are about rather than in a plan document that would drift from it.
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// WHAT THE REFUSAL ACTUALLY DISCOVERED: this repository has never been whole-tree
// strict-typechecked. The old floor prepared PER-ENTRY closures, so a broken module reddened only
// the closures importing it and was invisible elsewhere; a module nothing imported was never
// typechecked at all. This fold's admission wall is the first mechanism to demand the whole tree
// resolve as one namespace, and it cannot yet — a pre-existing concealment surfaced, not a
// regression, and a larger finding than the fold's wall-clock. The same asymmetry sits inside this
// lane's own verification: `gunbc compile --target dag` resolves a transitive import closure and
// reports the remaining 1,135 modules as census-only, which is why this cut's own six orphaned
// importers passed a whole-corpus compile and were caught only by the fold.
// ALL-OR-NOTHING PREPARATION IS UPHELD (ruling 2026-08-15), recorded because the temptation to
// relax it recurs. The subject IS the corpus. Admitting a partially-typechecking subject while
// reporting a whole-corpus verdict is the empty-observation narrow — rendering 'I could not admit
// this population' as 'this population is fine' — which DESIGN names as strictly worse than the
// widen section 5 already forbids: a widen is merely expensive, a narrow is silently uncovered.
// The ordering consequence is real and owned by the operator, not this lane: the fold's first
// green day requires the last pre-existing defect to be fixed.
// THE FOLD'S MEMORY ENVELOPE IS A FIRST-CLASS PROPERTY, measured rather than estimated. One run was
// OOM-KILLED (exit 137 at 537s, during strict preparation — a kill, not a refusal). The next run,
// sampled: RSS climbed 3.9 -> 4.8 -> 5.8 -> 6.3 GiB over 90 seconds and PEAKED AT 9.37 GiB. The
// container cap (31 GiB) is not the binding constraint; the shared 20 GiB slice is, and the kernel
// kills the LARGEST task in it when the aggregate is exceeded — the likely cause of the kill.
// CONSEQUENCE, which is what ranks this beside the diagnostic census: non-deterministic OOM on a
// shared host is exactly what makes a required check flaky, and a check that dies for reasons
// unrelated to its subject teaches everyone to ignore it. DIRECTION RULED, MEASUREMENT NOT
// STARTED: do NOT re-shard into batches or worker processes to make it fit — that is the deleted
// decomposition rebuilding itself inside the replacement one convenience at a time; the single
// prepared subject is precisely what the fold exists to be. The 9.37 GiB peak is a cost-shape
// defect to root-cause (the repository already carries that lane: schedule-derived per-module
// retention, and the carrier/interning roots), not a reason to change the shape.
// THE FOLD'S MEMORY ENVELOPE IS A FIRST-CLASS PROPERTY, measured rather than estimated. One run was
// OOM-KILLED (exit 137 at 537s, during strict preparation — a kill, not a refusal). The next run
// was sampled: RSS climbed 3.9 -> 4.8 -> 5.8 -> 6.3 GiB over 90 seconds and PEAKED AT 9.37 GiB. The
// container cap (31 GiB) is not the binding constraint; the shared 20 GiB slice is, and the kernel
// kills the LARGEST task in it when the aggregate is exceeded, which is the likely cause of the
// kill. CONSEQUENCE, named because it is what makes this rank beside the diagnostic census:
// non-deterministic OOM on a shared host is exactly the shape that makes a required check flaky,
// and a check that dies for reasons unrelated to its subject teaches everyone to ignore it.
// DIRECTION RULED, MEASUREMENT NOT STARTED: do NOT re-shard into batches or worker processes to
// make it fit — that is the deleted decomposition rebuilding itself inside the replacement one
// convenience at a time, and the single prepared subject is precisely what the fold exists to be.
// The 9.37 GiB peak is a cost-shape defect to root-cause (the repository already carries that lane:
// schedule-derived per-module retention, and the carrier/interning roots), not a reason to change
// the shape.
fn expected_witness_floor_yml() -> WitnessFloorGenerationOutcome {
  match admit_workflow_toolchain_homes(workflow: witness_floor_workflow) {
    WorkflowToolchainHomeRefused { job_id: j, refusal: r } =>
      WitnessFloorGenerationRefused { reason: toolchain_home_refusal_reason(job_id: j, refusal: r) }
    WorkflowToolchainHomesAdmitted =>
      if witness_floor_capability_closure_holds() && build_lane_capability_closure_holds() && heal_generated_artifacts_capability_closure_holds() && required_lanes_gate_is_renderable() && required_ci_measurement_scripts_are_renderable() {
        WitnessFloorGenerated {
          content: serialize_yaml(v: project_workflow_to_yaml(workflow: witness_floor_workflow))
        }
      } else {
        WitnessFloorGenerationRefused { reason: witness_floor_capability_closure_refusal_reason }
      }
  }
}
